WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.600
Today's lecture will focus
on genetic evaluations

00:00:08.600 --> 00:00:10.560
and long horizon tasks.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:15.280
How many of you have had to run
an evaluation for your project

00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:17.140
to do whatever
you've been doing?

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:22.580
No one is running an evaluation.

00:00:22.580 --> 00:00:26.080
How are you guys
doing any projects?

00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:29.260
What evaluation tasks have you
used for agentic evaluations?

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:38.880
I'm going to call people
out if I don't get answers.

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:42.160
What have you been
using in your project?

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.400
I wonder for agentic
evaluation, do you

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.800
mean like it has to be something
like a [INAUDIBLE] bench

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:50.280
or knowledge task or
body of knowledge task?

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:51.380
All of those count.

00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:51.880
OK.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:55.060
Yeah, so then, I guess,
like [INAUDIBLE].

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:58.160
OK, so that's a multi-hop
question answering task.

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:01.080
So [INAUDIBLE] bench
would also count.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:02.740
What else have you
folks been using?

00:01:07.240 --> 00:01:09.240
There's no other answers.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.920
What about you?

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:12.460
Yeah.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.900
I'm working on a private
Wiki for my employer.

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:21.440
And I sat down and
make notes out of that

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:23.240
and then feed that
into a coding agent.

00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:25.080
OK, nice.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:27.360
It's a private Wiki
question/answers.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:30.400
So I think a lot of this
lecture will focus on

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.640
what is challenging
about agentic evaluations

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.320
relative to where
things have been before

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:38.440
and how things become harder.

00:01:38.440 --> 00:01:40.800
The papers that we'll
cover to cover this topic

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.680
will be focused on measuring
the ability of these models

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:48.430
to complete long
tasks in terms of,

00:01:48.430 --> 00:01:49.680
what's the length of the task?

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.520
Length, as in time
horizon of the task

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:54.720
that the models can complete.

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:57.200
Also second paper
that we'll look at

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.480
is GDPVal which was a
recent benchmark that

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:01.000
was released by OpenAI.

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:03.240
This will focus on
how the models perform

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:05.098
on real-world, economically
valuable tasks.

00:02:05.098 --> 00:02:06.640
So there has been
a lot of discussion

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:12.000
around the models can do tasks
that real-world professionals

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:12.660
can do.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:15.720
So this is measuring that
in a very real sense.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:17.280
And then the last
task will very much

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.500
focus on deep
research style tasks.

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:20.980
So it is called
DeepScholar-Bench.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:22.140
It's out of Stanford.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:25.120
And it's a live benchmark that
focus on generative research

00:02:25.120 --> 00:02:25.700
synthesis.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:27.440
So it takes a bunch
of papers and then

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:30.480
tries to write the related
work section, a task

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.840
that I have always wanted
the language models to go

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.120
do for the papers that I write.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:38.280
So this actually measures
how good of a job

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:41.540
does the model do in
solving these kind of tasks?

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.120
So one of the
challenges of measuring

00:02:46.120 --> 00:02:48.720
intelligence or progress
of AI is that we

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:54.300
have to measure what matters.

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:55.860
How are the models progressing?

00:02:55.860 --> 00:02:58.900
So when we started about
four or five years ago,

00:02:58.900 --> 00:03:01.560
even the fact that the models
could generate any explanations

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:03.360
was such a big deal.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:06.880
And now we measure
the progress of AI

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:10.160
in terms of whether
we can forecast

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:13.940
how it will influence economics
or how it will influence safety.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:17.480
Traditional benchmarks,
which involve chatbots

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:20.160
or which involve, say,
question/answering or single

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:24.040
shot just answering something
that was already in the context

00:03:24.040 --> 00:03:26.420
are starting to saturate
it fairly quickly.

00:03:26.420 --> 00:03:28.880
However, we do need to
measure, both in terms

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:33.120
of capability and economic
impact, how these models are

00:03:33.120 --> 00:03:36.080
progressing so that we can
see what their impact will

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:38.400
be in the coming years.

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:42.440
So in terms of
measuring capability,

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:46.120
what we covered today is we are
looking at the task, of how long

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:50.440
or how complex of a task
can these models accomplish?

00:03:50.440 --> 00:03:52.980
And the second
question we ask is,

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:55.120
what is the economic
value of the task

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:56.940
that these models can do?

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:00.080
Or can they do real-world tasks
that humans can currently do?

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:03.200
And the two papers that
we are referencing here

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:05.760
is-- or the two metrics
that we are referencing here

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:10.040
is that METR refers to the
time horizon metric calibrated

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:12.520
by human professionals.

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:16.399
And GDPVal compares the win
rate of model performing

00:04:16.399 --> 00:04:19.000
a task relative to experts.

00:04:19.000 --> 00:04:23.240
So both of them are equally
valid metrics in certain ways.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:24.660
And the insights
are very similar.

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:28.760
But the trends that they measure
in terms of how models perform

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:32.240
are quite different.

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:35.280
So let's start
with the first one.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:38.660
So, when you're
talking to a chatbot,

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:41.710
if you were talking to a
chatbot, say three, four,

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:46.200
or five years ago, usually
after a first couple of rounds

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:48.500
in multi-turn conversation,
the model will be lost.

00:04:48.500 --> 00:04:52.200
It doesn't keep context of what
you were talking to it about.

00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:54.260
Today as you talk
to these models,

00:04:54.260 --> 00:04:56.310
the conversation
can go pretty long.

00:04:56.310 --> 00:04:58.390
So it does keep that context.

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:01.170
And that relates then
to the question of,

00:05:01.170 --> 00:05:04.850
what is the duration of the task
that the model can complete.

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:06.750
So that's just the
duration question.

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:09.670
And then the second question is,
what's the percentage success?

00:05:09.670 --> 00:05:11.810
Can it do so reliably?

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:16.010
So METR, this
particular benchmark

00:05:16.010 --> 00:05:18.790
focuses on duration of the
task that model can complete.

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:20.970
And then it associates a
reliability metric of like,

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:24.410
will it complete it
with 50% success or will

00:05:24.410 --> 00:05:26.490
it complete it with 80% success?

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:29.170
And so on.

00:05:29.170 --> 00:05:32.890
And the universal
anchor for these tasks

00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:34.810
ends up being the,
how much time will it

00:05:34.810 --> 00:05:38.970
take humans to complete
if they were professionals

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:42.010
in solving these tasks?

00:05:42.010 --> 00:05:44.170
So there are three
task suites that

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:48.570
are used to design this
particular benchmark.

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:50.030
One is extremely small.

00:05:50.030 --> 00:05:51.430
So one to 30 seconds.

00:05:51.430 --> 00:05:53.750
It basically focusing
on just atomic actions.

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:56.270
So in software engineering,
can you open a file?

00:05:56.270 --> 00:05:58.970
Can you do something small?

00:05:58.970 --> 00:06:03.630
The second one is HCAST,
which focuses on more 1 minute

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:05.890
to 30 hours style of
tasks, which is basically

00:06:05.890 --> 00:06:09.370
talking about different software
and research engineering tasks.

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:13.090
And then finally, RE-Bench
that's actually giving a full ML

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:17.110
research style tasks, which can
go on as long as eight hours.

00:06:17.110 --> 00:06:19.810
So you've seen like AI scientist
paper covered in this class

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:20.530
before.

00:06:20.530 --> 00:06:23.890
So similar style of benchmarks.

00:06:23.890 --> 00:06:27.210
In total, it covers
about 170 tasks

00:06:27.210 --> 00:06:30.290
going all the way from say,
one seconds to eight hours.

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:34.750
So if you were to look at
this, in terms of a division,

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:37.930
you will see that HCAST
has about 97 tasks.

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:41.150
These are the 1 minute
to 30 hour tasks.

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:44.160
SWAA has about 66 tasks.

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.410
And then RE-Bench, which are
more research engineering

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:49.570
tasks that take much longer
are about seven tasks.

00:06:49.570 --> 00:06:52.070
So the first they do is
they collect these tasks,

00:06:52.070 --> 00:06:54.330
they vet the set of
tasks that go into each

00:06:54.330 --> 00:06:56.530
of these benchmark suites.

00:06:56.530 --> 00:07:00.570
Then they actually have time
estimates from both human

00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:02.810
doing the task and then
the agent doing the task.

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:06.010
From human runs, they actually
estimate the amount of time

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:07.910
it takes the human to
complete that task,

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:10.050
and whether they found
it difficult or not.

00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:12.650
And then for the agent,
they're measuring whether they

00:07:12.650 --> 00:07:14.850
completed it reliably or not.

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:18.110
And finally, based on
enough data points,

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:21.090
they fit a curve which measures
the success rate versus the time

00:07:21.090 --> 00:07:22.010
estimate.

00:07:22.010 --> 00:07:24.410
And based on that, they
find the time horizon

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:27.090
for these models
completing the tasks.

00:07:27.090 --> 00:07:31.050
So that gives a sense of the
horizon length relative to model

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:31.830
release dates.

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:33.970
So what we'll see
in terms of trends

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:38.050
are, for models released
on different dates, roughly

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:41.850
what is the time horizon with
which the models complete

00:07:41.850 --> 00:07:42.490
the tasks?

00:07:42.490 --> 00:07:47.130
50% of the time or
ti 80% of the time?

00:07:47.130 --> 00:07:47.890
Questions.

00:07:54.570 --> 00:07:59.030
What is a task that takes
you guys a couple of minutes?

00:07:59.030 --> 00:08:01.970
And what's a task that takes
you guys say a couple of hours

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:04.910
that you think you would like
to delegate to the models?

00:08:12.770 --> 00:08:15.210
Writing the literature
review section of the paper.

00:08:15.210 --> 00:08:15.930
Yes.

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:20.090
That usually takes
several hours.

00:08:20.090 --> 00:08:21.950
So that would be a great task.

00:08:21.950 --> 00:08:28.410
And that would fall somewhere
in the HCAST or RE-Bench task.

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:32.330
Any other takers
relative to what you do?

00:08:32.330 --> 00:08:33.962
Yeah.

00:08:33.962 --> 00:08:36.690
[INAUDIBLE] for each reference.

00:08:36.690 --> 00:08:37.409
Say that again.

00:08:37.409 --> 00:08:41.289
Double-check [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:41.289 --> 00:08:44.490
So in the paper writing,
you want the BibTeX item

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:45.230
to be correct.

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:47.770
So you want it to go
query the right websites

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:49.810
and find the BibTeX item.

00:08:49.810 --> 00:08:53.030
How many minutes do you
think that should take?

00:08:53.030 --> 00:08:55.830
So it falls more in that
one-- the HCAST style,

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:57.370
like diverse tasks?

00:08:57.370 --> 00:08:59.810
OK.

00:08:59.810 --> 00:09:00.350
Cool.

00:09:02.930 --> 00:09:05.850
Any other tasks that people
think that the model should

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:07.350
be good at?

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:14.770
OK.

00:09:14.770 --> 00:09:26.130
I think [INAUDIBLE]
should be faster.

00:09:26.130 --> 00:09:29.650
So refactoring the codebase
can be large or can be small.

00:09:29.650 --> 00:09:32.420
Yeah, it depends on
what the refractor.

00:09:32.420 --> 00:09:33.550
What the refactor requires.

00:09:33.550 --> 00:09:36.110
If the logic stays the same and
it's just moving around code,

00:09:36.110 --> 00:09:39.230
then that perhaps is a few
hours can be a couple of days.

00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:41.970
Someone has to check
correctness of that as well.

00:09:41.970 --> 00:09:43.130
Makes sense.

00:09:43.130 --> 00:09:45.683
So that one will fall perhaps
like closer to RE-Bench.

00:09:45.683 --> 00:09:47.850
One can imagine that there
will be a version of just

00:09:47.850 --> 00:09:50.930
like the coding-related tasks.

00:09:50.930 --> 00:09:52.810
Cool.

00:09:52.810 --> 00:09:53.310
OK.

00:09:57.290 --> 00:10:01.970
So for the second step,
for the human [INAUDIBLE],

00:10:01.970 --> 00:10:06.130
so for a certain type of task,
for these types [INAUDIBLE]

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:09.410
human beings [INAUDIBLE]
some people do it faster.

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:12.170
Some people do it slower.

00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:18.070
So I'm just wondering,
so on one extreme case,

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:21.890
you just have one main evaluator
and then you have just one

00:10:21.890 --> 00:10:22.930
[INAUDIBLE].

00:10:22.930 --> 00:10:26.173
On the other extreme,
you have 100 generations.

00:10:26.173 --> 00:10:27.090
You have distribution.

00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:30.350
And you can compare where
the algorithm stands.

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:32.290
So I'm just curious
where that's most

00:10:32.290 --> 00:10:33.890
of the individual performance.

00:10:33.890 --> 00:10:36.370
So typically, what is
done with most of the--

00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:39.970
I mean, getting a lot of experts
to solve the task is relatively

00:10:39.970 --> 00:10:40.590
expensive.

00:10:40.590 --> 00:10:43.130
So typically, you will have
a few experts solve the task

00:10:43.130 --> 00:10:45.570
and then you look for
inter-rater agreement

00:10:45.570 --> 00:10:48.110
or inter-human agreement
in this particular case.

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:49.830
And what you're saying
is that generally,

00:10:49.830 --> 00:10:52.050
if the numbers are
very far off, then

00:10:52.050 --> 00:10:53.630
you go get more data points.

00:10:53.630 --> 00:10:58.530
And if there's general
agreement, then you move on.

00:10:58.530 --> 00:11:00.530
So just the example
of task, just related

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:03.770
to what the class talked about,
where you wanted the literature

00:11:03.770 --> 00:11:07.250
review section or
you wanted the BibTeX

00:11:07.250 --> 00:11:11.370
being accurately classified
or the refactoring

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:12.430
of the code base.

00:11:12.430 --> 00:11:14.450
Some of the example tasks
of different durations

00:11:14.450 --> 00:11:17.670
that were in this paper
are find a shell script.

00:11:17.670 --> 00:11:20.730
So that's perhaps just
take three seconds.

00:11:20.730 --> 00:11:22.270
Or Wikipedia research.

00:11:22.270 --> 00:11:26.490
This is closer to the research
section that you wanted.

00:11:26.490 --> 00:11:30.330
Or another one is detect and
fix a bug in the input files

00:11:30.330 --> 00:11:35.050
for a simulation, so that takes
like 10 minutes for a human.

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:36.590
Then there is munging of data.

00:11:36.590 --> 00:11:38.010
So data munging
task where you're

00:11:38.010 --> 00:11:40.930
transforming some JSON data
from one format to another.

00:11:40.930 --> 00:11:44.850
And then Cuda backtesting
tool, which basically you

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:46.190
are implementing.

00:11:46.190 --> 00:11:48.850
Custom Cuda kernels
can take tens of hours

00:11:48.850 --> 00:11:51.210
to here it's like
eight hours and they

00:11:51.210 --> 00:11:54.570
are aiming for a certain
performance improvement.

00:11:54.570 --> 00:12:00.050
So all of these tests are part
of this particular benchmark.

00:12:00.050 --> 00:12:02.090
And the way-- this
actually addresses

00:12:02.090 --> 00:12:03.850
the question you
were asking, the way

00:12:03.850 --> 00:12:07.090
they are going about getting
the human baseline for task

00:12:07.090 --> 00:12:10.170
completion is that
they go and get

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:13.010
skilled professionals with
roughly five years of experience

00:12:13.010 --> 00:12:15.050
in the field to record
their completion

00:12:15.050 --> 00:12:16.910
times for successful attempts.

00:12:16.910 --> 00:12:19.850
And then they take a geometric
mean for the task difficulty

00:12:19.850 --> 00:12:22.667
rating based on the humans
that went and solved this.

00:12:22.667 --> 00:12:24.250
One challenge with
this is that people

00:12:24.250 --> 00:12:27.330
who are experienced in the
field are conditioned to what it

00:12:27.330 --> 00:12:28.750
means to be successful in this.

00:12:28.750 --> 00:12:30.810
So they will often
underestimate the difficulty

00:12:30.810 --> 00:12:32.650
of the task, which
might not correlate

00:12:32.650 --> 00:12:37.530
to how the model sees whether
this task is difficult or not.

00:12:37.530 --> 00:12:41.110
So in terms of trends, what
they found is, on the x-axis,

00:12:41.110 --> 00:12:42.710
we have the human
time to complete.

00:12:42.710 --> 00:12:44.820
And on the y-axis, we
have the success rate

00:12:44.820 --> 00:12:47.500
going all the way from 0 to 1.

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:51.100
So the model success rate for--

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:55.060
if you look at smaller tasks
like 1 to 30 second tasks, which

00:12:55.060 --> 00:12:58.560
are the SWAA tasks
or the green tasks,

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:00.340
that's generally
on the higher side.

00:13:00.340 --> 00:13:02.940
For HCAST tasks,
which are basically

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:05.780
the tasks which are diverse
tasks going all the way from

00:13:05.780 --> 00:13:08.740
say few minutes
to several hours,

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:10.000
that's all over the place.

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:15.100
So it's anywhere from 0.8 being
the highest all the way to 0.

00:13:15.100 --> 00:13:18.000
And then the RE-Bench
tasks have some success,

00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:21.340
but tend to be on the lower
side at this point in time.

00:13:21.340 --> 00:13:24.940
There have been papers saying
that they are making progress

00:13:24.940 --> 00:13:26.460
on RE-Bench tasks,
but generally,

00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:30.760
it's a much harder task in terms
of the human time to complete,

00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:31.700
it takes long.

00:13:31.700 --> 00:13:34.740
And then the model success
rate is also fairly low.

00:13:34.740 --> 00:13:36.380
So the longer the
task, the harder

00:13:36.380 --> 00:13:38.960
it's for models to stay
on track and complete.

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:43.340
And this is partly why agentic
evaluations and what you measure

00:13:43.340 --> 00:13:46.820
starts to become a
challenging aspect.

00:13:46.820 --> 00:13:49.140
In terms of trends,
if you were to--

00:13:49.140 --> 00:13:51.620
we chatted about
the fact that if you

00:13:51.620 --> 00:13:57.300
were to query these models
about 2019 when GPT-2 came out,

00:13:57.300 --> 00:14:00.540
typically, the models would
really just address tasks

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:01.840
that were two seconds long.

00:14:01.840 --> 00:14:05.060
So on the y-axis, you have
the task time for completion

00:14:05.060 --> 00:14:06.640
with 50% success rate.

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:09.020
So it doesn't have to
be completely reliable.

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:14.820
So in 2019 when GPT-2 came
out, only two seconds.

00:14:14.820 --> 00:14:18.740
Closer to GPT-4,
which is 2023, you're

00:14:18.740 --> 00:14:20.540
starting to see a few minutes.

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:25.640
And when we have Claude 3.7
Sonnet and o1, at that point,

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:27.820
you're starting to
see a few hours.

00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:31.020
Now note that this is
almost like a doubling

00:14:31.020 --> 00:14:32.940
because this is--

00:14:32.940 --> 00:14:35.082
so it's doubling
every seven months.

00:14:35.082 --> 00:14:36.540
But the other thing
to note here is

00:14:36.540 --> 00:14:38.960
that even though these
tasks are being completed,

00:14:38.960 --> 00:14:40.500
the reliability is not high.

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:43.080
They only being completed
50% of the time.

00:14:43.080 --> 00:14:46.560
So that's like saying that you
gave your task to an intern,

00:14:46.560 --> 00:14:48.520
but it only completes
it 50% of the time.

00:14:48.520 --> 00:14:51.740
So it might come back with
an answer or it might not.

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:55.940
So there's always some
amount of uncertainty.

00:14:55.940 --> 00:14:57.980
So this is just
making the same point

00:14:57.980 --> 00:15:01.460
that going from GPT-2, which was
just two seconds to GPT-4, which

00:15:01.460 --> 00:15:05.800
was like eight minutes, now
Claude 3.7 Sonnet in 2025

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:10.460
can go all the way to 59
minutes with 50% success rate.

00:15:10.460 --> 00:15:13.280
And what is really
driving this improvement?

00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:15.500
So some of the
capabilities that we're

00:15:15.500 --> 00:15:18.660
seeing in the model that
are really helpful in terms

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:22.340
