00:20:18.460 --> 00:20:21.520
So now let's take a look at
reliability aspect of it.

00:20:21.520 --> 00:20:24.540
So capability is
only one axis along

00:20:24.540 --> 00:20:26.240
which we want these
models to do better.

00:20:26.240 --> 00:20:27.860
Reliability is the other one.

00:20:27.860 --> 00:20:30.580
So what we are
measuring here is again

00:20:30.580 --> 00:20:32.240
x-axis is the
model release date.

00:20:32.240 --> 00:20:35.760
And now we want to see how the
models do at 80% success rate.

00:20:35.760 --> 00:20:38.760
So the gray line is the
50% reliability one.

00:20:38.760 --> 00:20:42.340
And then the blue line
is the 80% success rate.

00:20:42.340 --> 00:20:46.100
And y-axis is the time
taken by the models

00:20:46.100 --> 00:20:48.020
to complete the tasks.

00:20:48.020 --> 00:20:50.700
So what you see here is
that while we were claiming

00:20:50.700 --> 00:20:54.540
that you can get all the
way up to 59 minutes in 2025

00:20:54.540 --> 00:20:57.540
with the latest and
greatest models, if you want

00:20:57.540 --> 00:21:00.300
high reliability, you are
still at a few minutes

00:21:00.300 --> 00:21:02.520
or maybe tens of minutes.

00:21:02.520 --> 00:21:06.040
And so to get 80% success
rate with the models,

00:21:06.040 --> 00:21:07.900
there is a big delta
between the 59 minutes

00:21:07.900 --> 00:21:10.380
that we were claiming
with the best model

00:21:10.380 --> 00:21:12.700
versus at 80%
success rate, you're

00:21:12.700 --> 00:21:17.080
still closer to say, somewhere
around 8 to 10 minutes.

00:21:17.080 --> 00:21:19.740
So big delta roughly
tells you that there's

00:21:19.740 --> 00:21:22.180
a lot of headroom to make
in terms of reliability

00:21:22.180 --> 00:21:26.000
so that the model comes back and
is successful most of the time.

00:21:30.020 --> 00:21:31.670
Yes.

00:21:31.670 --> 00:21:35.148
So do you [INAUDIBLE]
based on your experience?

