00:22:18.010 --> 00:22:20.430
So this gap, basically,
says that if you're

00:22:20.430 --> 00:22:24.590
trying to deploy these models
in real-world tasks, then

00:22:24.590 --> 00:22:26.390
there will be reliability
challenges even

00:22:26.390 --> 00:22:27.970
for moderately complex tasks.

00:22:27.970 --> 00:22:30.190
So that requires--
there's definitely

00:22:30.190 --> 00:22:32.310
a lot of headroom in
how we build systems

00:22:32.310 --> 00:22:35.430
on top of these models.

00:22:35.430 --> 00:22:37.370
And an important
question to ask is,

00:22:37.370 --> 00:22:39.510
what are the kind
of failures that we

00:22:39.510 --> 00:22:41.050
run into with these models?

00:22:41.050 --> 00:22:43.770
And this is looking
at both GPT-4,

00:22:43.770 --> 00:22:45.710
which is a non-reasoning
model and o1, which

00:22:45.710 --> 00:22:46.810
is a reasoning model.

00:22:46.810 --> 00:22:48.990
Now this paper is a bit
older and you can refer

00:22:48.990 --> 00:22:50.690
to things that are a bit newer.

00:22:50.690 --> 00:22:53.470
But at the same time,
the kind of failure modes

00:22:53.470 --> 00:22:55.650
are very similar in many cases.

00:22:55.650 --> 00:22:56.890
So we can take a look here.

00:22:56.890 --> 00:22:58.870
So the kind of failure
modes that they found

00:22:58.870 --> 00:23:01.850
are closer to either
poor planning.

00:23:01.850 --> 00:23:04.410
So the model doesn't know
how to break the task.

00:23:04.410 --> 00:23:07.150
It was given down into steps.

00:23:07.150 --> 00:23:08.250
Poor tool choice.

00:23:08.250 --> 00:23:11.510
So the set of tools that
it tries to choose here

00:23:11.510 --> 00:23:13.010
may or may not be correct.

00:23:13.010 --> 00:23:19.030
And there were errors along both
these axes in the GPT-4 and o1.

00:23:19.030 --> 00:23:21.350
A second failure type
is incorrect mental math

00:23:21.350 --> 00:23:22.270
or reasoning.

00:23:22.270 --> 00:23:23.250
Style tasks.

00:23:23.250 --> 00:23:25.630
So here the model
is basically doing

00:23:25.630 --> 00:23:29.550
some sort of math or reasoning,
but it's doing it incorrectly.

00:23:29.550 --> 00:23:31.910
Another failure type
is that prematurely, it

00:23:31.910 --> 00:23:34.078
abandons the tasks
because it goes

00:23:34.078 --> 00:23:35.870
around some repetitive
behavior, but is not

00:23:35.870 --> 00:23:38.430
quite able to figure out
what it means for the task

00:23:38.430 --> 00:23:40.990
to be successful.

00:23:40.990 --> 00:23:44.350
Another failure mode is that
the model will basically

00:23:44.350 --> 00:23:46.470
get into a repetitive
loop, so it will basically

00:23:46.470 --> 00:23:47.490
go do some actions.

00:23:47.490 --> 00:23:50.270
And then that's still the
highest probability action

00:23:50.270 --> 00:23:51.570
even after it has failed.

00:23:51.570 --> 00:23:54.070
So it then goes and
does that action again.

00:23:54.070 --> 00:23:56.630
So it might basically
be with high probability

00:23:56.630 --> 00:23:57.810
repeating the same action.

00:23:57.810 --> 00:24:00.190
So with GPT-4, this
was a pretty high.

00:24:00.190 --> 00:24:03.030
For o1, this has become lower.

00:24:03.030 --> 00:24:06.390
And then there's other category
of tasks that they showed--

00:24:06.390 --> 00:24:08.390
there are other categories
of failures that they

00:24:08.390 --> 00:24:10.170
showed are also prevalent.

00:24:10.170 --> 00:24:12.150
But this gives you
a sense of planning,

00:24:12.150 --> 00:24:16.030
tool choice,
reasoning errors, not

00:24:16.030 --> 00:24:19.492
being able to figure out
when the task is completed,

00:24:19.492 --> 00:24:20.950
and then just
repetitive loops tend

00:24:20.950 --> 00:24:23.870
to be some of the failure
modes for these models.

00:24:23.870 --> 00:24:26.870
And failure mode analysis is
very useful in understanding

00:24:26.870 --> 00:24:29.210
where the models can
or cannot improve.

00:24:32.030 --> 00:24:39.350
This is a common way, is
that typically [INAUDIBLE].

