SWE-bench has a similar
trend to what we were showing

00:26:10.190 --> 00:26:15.150
in all the plots before,
but the annotators generally

00:26:15.150 --> 00:26:16.570
underestimates SWE-bench.

00:26:16.570 --> 00:26:19.530
And the models also have seen
most of the GitHub repositories.

00:26:19.530 --> 00:26:22.950
So a lot of the estimates
that are made on SWE-bench

00:26:22.950 --> 00:26:25.710
tend to be shorter in
terms of doubling time

00:26:25.710 --> 00:26:30.190
relative to if it was a
completely unseen repository.

00:26:30.190 --> 00:26:33.470
A third challenge
with the benchmark

00:26:33.470 --> 00:26:37.510
is that if you actually have
internal pull requests-- so this

00:26:37.510 --> 00:26:40.710
is for internal code bases,
then what has been found

00:26:40.710 --> 00:26:42.537
is that if you put
contractors on the job,

00:26:42.537 --> 00:26:44.870
people who don't have context,
who have not been working

00:26:44.870 --> 00:26:48.410
on that code base, versus you
put code maintainers on the job.

00:26:48.410 --> 00:26:52.110
The contractors can be anywhere
from 5 to 18 times slower.

00:26:52.110 --> 00:26:54.710
And model performance
is consistent

00:26:54.710 --> 00:26:57.350
closer to contractor times
because it's basically never

00:26:57.350 --> 00:26:58.950
seen that code base before.

00:26:58.950 --> 00:27:02.950
So this suggests that when
you're dealing with these models

00:27:02.950 --> 00:27:06.162
going and solving these tasks,
this is still very valuable.

00:27:06.162 --> 00:27:07.870
But at the same time,
it's operating more

00:27:07.870 --> 00:27:11.150
as not someone who is
an expert in that area

00:27:11.150 --> 00:27:13.170
or has the context of that area.

00:27:13.170 --> 00:27:16.350
It's more as a low
context human who's

00:27:16.350 --> 00:27:18.870
basically been asked
to solve that problem,

00:27:18.870 --> 00:27:21.790
similar to a contractor.

00:27:21.790 --> 00:27:25.790
So that's summarizing
the first paper.

00:27:25.790 --> 00:27:27.910
But hopefully, that
gives you a flavor

00:27:27.910 --> 00:27:30.310
of when we are measuring
the progress in AI,

00:27:30.310 --> 00:27:32.310
one of the axes in
agentic evaluations

00:27:32.310 --> 00:27:34.910
that becomes important
is to measure,

00:27:34.910 --> 00:27:40.590
how long are the tasks that
the AI can complete reliably?

00:27:40.590 --> 00:27:43.810
Any questions or comments before
we move to the next section?

00:27:51.230 --> 00:27:53.247
OK.

00:27:53.247 --> 00:27:54.830
So the next paper
that we will look at

00:27:54.830 --> 00:27:57.310
is GDPVal, which is
evaluating the model

00:27:57.310 --> 00:28:01.630
performance on real-world
economically valuable tasks.

00:28:01.630 --> 00:28:05.690
So instead of asking the
question, can AI do this?

00:28:05.690 --> 00:28:08.750
Where you have a task that
you want to delegate to AI.

00:28:08.750 --> 00:28:11.230
Hear the question
is very much around,

00:28:11.230 --> 00:28:14.710
if we were to give this task to
the model instead of a human,

00:28:14.710 --> 00:28:16.150
is the output good enough?

00:28:16.150 --> 00:28:18.470
And the win rate
will be directly

00:28:18.470 --> 00:28:20.470
against industry experts.

00:28:20.470 --> 00:28:23.550
So it will actually take--
the task suite is designed

00:28:23.550 --> 00:28:25.190
as real work from
professionals who

00:28:25.190 --> 00:28:29.710
have more than a decade of
experience in their fields.

00:28:29.710 --> 00:28:32.190
So you will see here that
the kind of professions

00:28:32.190 --> 00:28:34.510
that were used for
task suite design

00:28:34.510 --> 00:28:36.010
are pretty broad ranging here.

00:28:36.010 --> 00:28:38.590
So it goes anywhere
from say real estate,

00:28:38.590 --> 00:28:42.070
where you take a broad
range of tasks to government

00:28:42.070 --> 00:28:44.870
to manufacturing,
professional, scientific,

00:28:44.870 --> 00:28:48.490
and technical services,
healthcare, finance, retail,

00:28:48.490 --> 00:28:51.910
wholesale, and information,
including film and video

00:28:51.910 --> 00:28:52.490
editors.

00:28:52.490 --> 00:28:54.610
So it's actually a pretty
broad range of tasks.

00:28:54.610 --> 00:28:56.830
And given the models
have become multimodal,

00:28:56.830 --> 00:29:00.430
this is does make sense.

00:29:00.430 --> 00:29:02.730
The models will not be
good at all of these tasks.

00:29:02.730 --> 00:29:06.150
They will only be good at some
of these tasks, of course.

00:29:06.150 --> 00:29:09.610
So let's take a look at
some example tasks here.

00:29:09.610 --> 00:29:12.270
And also think about
the tasks that you

00:29:12.270 --> 00:29:15.270
think that the models
might be good at as you

00:29:15.270 --> 00:29:16.630
go through this list.

00:29:16.630 --> 00:29:20.200
So in manufacturing engineer,
it's being asked to design a 3D

00:29:20.200 --> 00:29:24.080
model of a cable reel
stand for assembly line.

00:29:24.080 --> 00:29:26.540
In financial and
investment analysts,

00:29:26.540 --> 00:29:29.580
it's being asked to create
the competitive landscape.

00:29:29.580 --> 00:29:31.400
So it's a research heavy task.

00:29:31.400 --> 00:29:33.440
In registered nurse,
it's basically

00:29:33.440 --> 00:29:38.240
assessing the images of issue
and creating a consultation

00:29:38.240 --> 00:29:40.880
report.

00:29:40.880 --> 00:29:44.120
In film and video
editor, it's asking

00:29:44.120 --> 00:29:48.080
to create an intro reel
given some sort of script.

00:29:48.080 --> 00:29:50.600
In customer service,
it's being asked

00:29:50.600 --> 00:29:56.400
to draft an email response
for dissatisfied customer.

00:29:56.400 --> 00:29:58.920
In concierge, it's basically
creating an itinerary

00:29:58.920 --> 00:30:01.600
for a family of four.

00:30:01.600 --> 00:30:04.400
In audit, it's
basically figuring out

00:30:04.400 --> 00:30:08.680
pricing inconsistencies between
given a few different purchase

00:30:08.680 --> 00:30:09.480
orders.

00:30:09.480 --> 00:30:12.160
For a real estate agent, it's
designing a sales brochure

00:30:12.160 --> 00:30:13.480
for a new property.

00:30:13.480 --> 00:30:15.480
And then recreation
worker, it's basically

00:30:15.480 --> 00:30:22.960
asked to optimize the
layout of vendor fair?

00:30:22.960 --> 00:30:24.480
What do people think?

00:30:24.480 --> 00:30:27.440
These are very real
tasks, actually sourced

00:30:27.440 --> 00:30:30.560
from professionals
in their fields.

00:30:30.560 --> 00:30:32.992
Very interesting, right?

00:30:32.992 --> 00:30:36.680
May things are very
subjective to evaluate.

00:30:36.680 --> 00:30:38.740
Very subjective
to evaluate, yes.

00:30:38.740 --> 00:30:39.400
[INAUDIBLE]

00:30:39.400 --> 00:30:42.980
I mean, what I do is going
to may not be what you do.

00:30:42.980 --> 00:30:43.480
Yeah.

00:30:43.480 --> 00:30:46.320
So that's why the success
metric will be win rate.

00:30:46.320 --> 00:30:49.040
It's not like if you put
humans on the job versus you

00:30:49.040 --> 00:30:51.860
put models on the job,
what is the win rate?

00:30:51.860 --> 00:30:53.480
And there might
be specific style

00:30:53.480 --> 00:30:55.400
that the model chooses
that doesn't quite

00:30:55.400 --> 00:30:58.960
hold for all cases.

00:30:58.960 --> 00:31:00.280
Yes, so it's objective.

00:31:00.280 --> 00:31:02.920
And at the same time, that
subjective nature also

00:31:02.920 --> 00:31:05.080
makes one of the challenges.

00:31:05.080 --> 00:31:06.960
That's why real-world
work requires

00:31:06.960 --> 00:31:10.740
both context and subjectivity
in how things get evaluated.

00:31:13.328 --> 00:31:15.120
What are other real-world
tasks that people

00:31:15.120 --> 00:31:20.420
have seen that are
interesting related to this?

00:31:20.420 --> 00:31:23.120
You brought up about--
you put private Wiki

00:31:23.120 --> 00:31:29.720
QA and use that to query the
knowledge base in your job?

00:31:29.720 --> 00:31:33.280
That's feeding into coding even,
what we're finding is actually

00:31:33.280 --> 00:31:35.720
better finding dead
parts of the Wiki

00:31:35.720 --> 00:31:41.400
that are no longer
useful [INAUDIBLE].

00:31:41.400 --> 00:31:42.020
Thanks.

00:31:47.840 --> 00:31:51.120
Any other ideas from the class?

00:31:51.120 --> 00:31:53.200
[INAUDIBLE]

00:31:53.200 --> 00:31:54.600
Other tasks, yeah.

00:31:54.600 --> 00:31:58.440
So doctors-- no, I
mean, it has no respect.

00:31:58.440 --> 00:32:00.960
I feel for controversial
topics is not there.

00:32:00.960 --> 00:32:08.920
For example, [INAUDIBLE] or
other [INAUDIBLE] based on those

00:32:08.920 --> 00:32:12.840
context are following
requirements.

00:32:12.840 --> 00:32:15.140
So I think [INAUDIBLE] here.

00:32:15.140 --> 00:32:16.920
For example, there are tasks--

00:32:16.920 --> 00:32:18.982
I think we should separate out.

00:32:18.982 --> 00:32:21.440
There's definitely a question
around whether the models are

00:32:21.440 --> 00:32:25.160
good at it and whether we
want to use them for ethics

00:32:25.160 --> 00:32:27.988
and actual compliance reasons.

00:32:27.988 --> 00:32:30.280
So I think those two questions
need to be separated out

00:32:30.280 --> 00:32:34.040
from what models can do today.

00:32:34.040 --> 00:32:36.800
So this is more about,
is the model output

00:32:36.800 --> 00:32:39.680
good enough in the
subjective areas?

00:32:39.680 --> 00:32:42.320
I'm trying to see if
our doctors-- so there's

00:32:42.320 --> 00:32:44.400
registered nurses.

00:32:44.400 --> 00:32:45.500
There's a lot of tasks.

00:32:45.500 --> 00:32:47.840
But they put registered
nurses and nurse practitioners

00:32:47.840 --> 00:32:50.080
in healthcare more than doctors.

00:32:50.080 --> 00:32:51.640
What about for
people [INAUDIBLE]

00:32:51.640 --> 00:32:53.560
for the information
science, they

00:32:53.560 --> 00:32:56.760
don't mention like computation.

00:32:56.760 --> 00:32:57.760
Say that again.

00:32:57.760 --> 00:33:02.800
Compose a song, compose
a song for someone.

00:33:02.800 --> 00:33:04.480
OK.

00:33:04.480 --> 00:33:07.020
So yes, that's correct.

00:33:07.020 --> 00:33:09.300
So maybe that's an emerging
task that will show up.

00:33:09.300 --> 00:33:13.120
But that's not part of
this benchmark just yet.

00:33:13.120 --> 00:33:14.800
Here's one comment on that.

00:33:14.800 --> 00:33:17.260
Maybe for the music,
composing music,

00:33:17.260 --> 00:33:19.720
the verification is
quite subjective,

00:33:19.720 --> 00:33:23.920
or it's harder to say, I guess,
you can make [INAUDIBLE] music

00:33:23.920 --> 00:33:26.760
and all of them, it's
hard to differentiate.

00:33:26.760 --> 00:33:29.600
And maybe whether you're
trying to hear is it,

00:33:29.600 --> 00:33:32.160
so that they can--
there is a deliverable

00:33:32.160 --> 00:33:35.300
that's fairly clear if
it is working or not.

00:33:40.200 --> 00:33:45.280
So top-down, this dataset,
this evaluation dataset targets

00:33:45.280 --> 00:33:46.500
about nine sectors.

00:33:46.500 --> 00:33:49.518
And they actually went
for top 5% of the GDP.

00:33:49.518 --> 00:33:51.560
So they're asking, what
are the most economically

00:33:51.560 --> 00:33:52.360
valuable tasks?

00:33:52.360 --> 00:33:54.600
And whether the models
are good enough at that.

00:33:54.600 --> 00:34:00.080
They cover about 44 occupations
and sourced about 1,320 tasks

00:34:00.080 --> 00:34:01.120
through that.

00:34:01.120 --> 00:34:04.400
And 200 of them or 220
of them are actually

00:34:04.400 --> 00:34:07.120
in an open goal set that
is on hugging phase.

00:34:07.120 --> 00:34:09.940
Predominantly went
for digital tasks.

00:34:09.940 --> 00:34:14.679
So tasks on computer
which is 60% of the--

00:34:14.679 --> 00:34:20.000
so O*NET is a taxonomy of
all tasks that are related

00:34:20.000 --> 00:34:20.940
to occupations.

00:34:20.940 --> 00:34:25.719
So 60% of the O*NET tasks that
are computer-based and digital

00:34:25.719 --> 00:34:28.000
made it into this
evaluation dataset.

00:34:28.000 --> 00:34:32.360
And this basically is a
pretty representative coverage

00:34:32.360 --> 00:34:35.960
of various set of tasks
that an occupational expert

00:34:35.960 --> 00:34:39.920
will need to do in these
particular sectors.

00:34:39.920 --> 00:34:42.440
And in terms of the
characteristics of tasks

00:34:42.440 --> 00:34:45.159
that people went
after, they might

00:34:45.159 --> 00:34:48.500
have as long as seven hours
of completion time on average,

00:34:48.500 --> 00:34:52.040
but some might require weeks.

00:34:52.040 --> 00:34:54.699
The tasks were both
text-based and multimodal.

00:34:54.699 --> 00:34:58.240
So like CAD, video, audio,
spreadsheets, presentations.

00:34:58.240 --> 00:35:01.140
The average value of the task,
at least in the gold subset,

00:35:01.140 --> 00:35:04.960
was anywhere from
say $400 per task.

00:35:04.960 --> 00:35:07.240
But if anyone has
seen cvLancer, there's

00:35:07.240 --> 00:35:09.180
also much higher value tasks.

00:35:09.180 --> 00:35:12.720
So this one also has high
value and low value tasks.

00:35:12.720 --> 00:35:15.420
Many of these tasks,
about 70% of these tasks,

00:35:15.420 --> 00:35:17.660
require interaction
with the reference file.

00:35:17.660 --> 00:35:19.760
So you have to interact
with some reference files

00:35:19.760 --> 00:35:22.000
to arrive at a final output.

00:35:22.000 --> 00:35:26.380
And all of these tasks were
vetted through experts.

00:35:26.380 --> 00:35:30.880
So about 89% of the tasks were
actually vetted by experts

00:35:30.880 --> 00:35:32.300
as being very well specified.

00:35:32.300 --> 00:35:34.000
So they were not
like ill specified

00:35:34.000 --> 00:35:36.520
and extremely hard
for the model just

00:35:36.520 --> 00:35:38.720
because they're ill specified.

00:35:38.720 --> 00:35:42.660
So if you look at the
trend, so on the x-axis,

00:35:42.660 --> 00:35:46.680
now you see different models
all the way from GPT-4o to GPT-5

00:35:46.680 --> 00:35:49.680
high to Claude Opus 4.1.

00:35:49.680 --> 00:35:51.280
And on the y-axis,
you're looking

00:35:51.280 --> 00:35:55.100
at win rate versus the
industry professionals.

00:35:55.100 --> 00:35:56.600
So basically, if
you had an expert

00:35:56.600 --> 00:35:59.440
with more than a decade
of experience solving it

00:35:59.440 --> 00:36:01.800
versus the model solving it.

00:36:01.800 --> 00:36:05.680
So light blue is wins plus ties
and dark blue is wins only.

00:36:05.680 --> 00:36:07.840
There's a pairwise preferences.

00:36:07.840 --> 00:36:11.180
So if you look at
GPT-4o on an average,

00:36:11.180 --> 00:36:13.640
the average numbers don't
mean that every single task

00:36:13.640 --> 00:36:15.280
was solved like that.

00:36:15.280 --> 00:36:19.680
Go anywhere from say
12.4%, so that's 2024,

00:36:19.680 --> 00:36:22.180
to it starts to get to 25.

00:36:22.180 --> 00:36:24.460
And then more recently,
it is in the 30s.

00:36:24.460 --> 00:36:29.160
And then now with Claude Opus
4.1, it starts to get to 47.6.

00:36:29.160 --> 00:36:30.960
So that's a very
different trend compared

00:36:30.960 --> 00:36:33.920
to METR where we were talking
about this doubling trend

00:36:33.920 --> 00:36:35.260
every seven months.

00:36:35.260 --> 00:36:37.440
It's more of a
linear trend roughly

00:36:37.440 --> 00:36:38.840
compared to the
exponential trend

00:36:38.840 --> 00:36:40.640
that METR was talking about.

00:36:40.640 --> 00:36:43.320
And at the same time,
this is real work

00:36:43.320 --> 00:36:45.580
where you are comparing
relative to an expert.

00:36:45.580 --> 00:36:47.160
So the measurement
is very much based

00:36:47.160 --> 00:36:49.760
on whether this task, whether
the model output was good

00:36:49.760 --> 00:36:50.500
enough or not.

00:36:50.500 --> 00:36:54.040
So the reliability or rather
the model output being useful

00:36:54.040 --> 00:36:57.320
matters here.

00:36:57.320 --> 00:36:59.760
Questions.

00:36:59.760 --> 00:37:01.640
Yes.

00:37:01.640 --> 00:37:05.680
This trend is directly
comparable to the meter one,

00:37:05.680 --> 00:37:08.680
though, because
here the y-axis win

00:37:08.680 --> 00:37:15.960
rate for industry professionals
as opposed to the time horizon

00:37:15.960 --> 00:37:17.380
that's in the other case.

00:37:17.380 --> 00:37:22.040
So yeah, should I really think
about them as disagreeing?

00:37:22.040 --> 00:37:24.598
I wouldn't say that
disagreeing was wrong

00:37:24.598 --> 00:37:26.640
because they're measuring
different metric, as we

00:37:26.640 --> 00:37:27.660
said early on.

00:37:27.660 --> 00:37:30.640
But I think what it's
roughly emphasizing

00:37:30.640 --> 00:37:34.400
is that when you see
an exponential trend,

00:37:34.400 --> 00:37:38.618
people often assume that OK,
so now AI can do one hour.

00:37:38.618 --> 00:37:40.660
So next, it will be able
to do a couple of hours.

00:37:40.660 --> 00:37:42.940
And the next, it will be
able to do a couple of days.

00:37:42.940 --> 00:37:46.100
So there's that exponential
trend that gets people.

00:37:46.100 --> 00:37:47.520
What this is saying
is that if you

00:37:47.520 --> 00:37:51.840
look at tasks that require
say several hours or a couple

00:37:51.840 --> 00:37:55.400
of days or a couple of weeks,
then the models are only

00:37:55.400 --> 00:37:58.360
so reliable in doing that and
it's actually breaking it down

00:37:58.360 --> 00:37:59.220
by profession.

00:37:59.220 --> 00:38:01.240
So it kind of makes
it more realistic

00:38:01.240 --> 00:38:05.220
by nailing it down not
just by numbers of hours,

00:38:05.220 --> 00:38:07.180
but more by this is real work.

00:38:07.180 --> 00:38:09.110
And can the AI
models do it or not?

00:38:13.050 --> 00:38:13.730
Other comments?

00:38:13.730 --> 00:38:14.230
Yeah.

00:38:14.230 --> 00:38:17.850
I think this chart is like on
the aggregate level across all

00:38:17.850 --> 00:38:18.710
the professions.

00:38:18.710 --> 00:38:20.750
We will look at the
specific ones as well.

00:38:20.750 --> 00:38:23.550
So there might be maybe ones
that are more susceptible,

00:38:23.550 --> 00:38:24.410
where, I guess--

00:38:27.322 --> 00:38:30.190
Another set of comments
with respect to, say,

00:38:30.190 --> 00:38:32.490
the model-specific
trends is that it always

00:38:32.490 --> 00:38:36.310
tends to be better at, say,
aesthetics, document formatting,

00:38:36.310 --> 00:38:38.910
understanding PDFs,
spreadsheets, and presentations,

00:38:38.910 --> 00:38:42.690
while GPT-5 often tends to be
better at, say, instruction

00:38:42.690 --> 00:38:45.670
following, correct calculations,
and just text-based tasks.

00:38:45.670 --> 00:38:47.210
So that's something
that was noticed

00:38:47.210 --> 00:38:50.450
by this particular evaluation.

00:38:50.450 --> 00:38:52.690
And then if you look
at failure modes, which

00:38:52.690 --> 00:38:56.170
is very important to analyze in
most of these benchmarks, then

00:38:56.170 --> 00:38:58.250
oftentimes the
common area failure

00:38:58.250 --> 00:39:00.230
ends up being
instruction following.

00:39:00.230 --> 00:39:05.010
So a lot of the blue bars show
different instruction following

00:39:05.010 --> 00:39:07.650
errors in different models.

00:39:07.650 --> 00:39:10.570
Oftentimes, the
models will promise

00:39:10.570 --> 00:39:12.930
to look at the reference
data, but then actually not

00:39:12.930 --> 00:39:14.248
look at it.

00:39:14.248 --> 00:39:16.290
They will override it with
whatever hallucination

00:39:16.290 --> 00:39:17.690
they want to come up with.

00:39:17.690 --> 00:39:19.970
And then there's also
formatting errors

00:39:19.970 --> 00:39:23.730
that tend to be a
bit of a problem,

00:39:23.730 --> 00:39:25.970
with, GPT-5 tends to have
fewer instruction following

00:39:25.970 --> 00:39:30.490
error is what was measured
by this particular benchmark.

00:39:30.490 --> 00:39:33.690
Related to the reference data,
so our next particular section

00:39:33.690 --> 00:39:38.130
of the lecture will actually
discuss more about that, being

00:39:38.130 --> 00:39:40.930
able to consult a knowledge
base or reference data

00:39:40.930 --> 00:39:47.170
and why that's an
important skill to go test.

00:39:47.170 --> 00:39:50.070
And then in terms more
on the failure modes.

00:39:50.070 --> 00:39:53.673
So this was basically
measuring whether GPT-5,

00:39:53.673 --> 00:39:56.090
if you look at the percentage
of tasks that will generally

00:39:56.090 --> 00:39:59.010
be seen as acceptable but
subpar, if you delegated

00:39:59.010 --> 00:40:00.650
that task to the model.

00:40:00.650 --> 00:40:03.450
What they found was that
roughly half the tasks were

00:40:03.450 --> 00:40:07.450
acceptable, but subpar, but
then only 20% of the tasks

00:40:07.450 --> 00:40:13.010
fell somewhere like OK, the
model will genuinely be better.

00:40:13.010 --> 00:40:16.910
And 29% of the tasks were
like bad or catastrophic,

00:40:16.910 --> 00:40:20.210
where the model output was
really not acceptable at all.

00:40:20.210 --> 00:40:22.690
And there was some
amount of disagreement

00:40:22.690 --> 00:40:25.210
between human
graders with respect

00:40:25.210 --> 00:40:29.130
to whether they agree on what
the assessment of the model

00:40:29.130 --> 00:40:30.030
output is or not.

00:40:30.030 --> 00:40:34.730
So that also affects
some of this analysis.

00:40:34.730 --> 00:40:37.090
And then another
interesting thing

00:40:37.090 --> 00:40:38.930
is that it's something
that since this

00:40:38.930 --> 00:40:43.170
is a self-improving agent's
class, so a lot of times

00:40:43.170 --> 00:40:45.350
if you basically do
parallel sampling.

00:40:45.350 --> 00:40:47.290
So if you try something
or sequential sampling

00:40:47.290 --> 00:40:50.770
where you do something
n times, then

00:40:50.770 --> 00:40:53.690
ask the model to fix itself
based on whatever it was seeing.

00:40:53.690 --> 00:40:55.910
That loop can often
really improve.

00:40:55.910 --> 00:40:57.970
So what this particular
table is showing

00:40:57.970 --> 00:41:01.510
is that you can either
do naive just one trial

00:41:01.510 --> 00:41:03.390
or you can do-- sorry,
you can try one time

00:41:03.390 --> 00:41:06.970
or you can try n times where
you basically, every single time

00:41:06.970 --> 00:41:10.550
you do something, you can
improve the last iteration.

00:41:10.550 --> 00:41:12.650
So in terms of
speed improvement,

00:41:12.650 --> 00:41:18.330
what they found was that GPT-5
was overall about 1.6x in cost

00:41:18.330 --> 00:41:22.370
improvement if you could try
n times and about 1.4 in speed

00:41:22.370 --> 00:41:24.870
improvement relative
to an unaided expert.

00:41:24.870 --> 00:41:27.270
But there is still
where it's successful,

00:41:27.270 --> 00:41:30.150
it is still less than 10%
of the human expert salary.

00:41:30.150 --> 00:41:32.370
So there's definitely
some subset of professions

00:41:32.370 --> 00:41:35.930
where the models are
doing a pretty strong job

00:41:35.930 --> 00:41:41.490
and have the capability to
take on that tasks completely.

00:41:41.490 --> 00:41:44.690
So some people also mentioned
exactly which category

00:41:44.690 --> 00:41:45.670
We will get that.

00:41:48.530 --> 00:41:51.970
That's exactly
where we're going.

00:41:51.970 --> 00:41:54.530
So in terms of
performance variation.

00:41:54.530 --> 00:41:58.210
So by sectors, in government,
retail, and wholesale,

00:41:58.210 --> 00:42:00.770
I think the broad items,
the broad categories

00:42:00.770 --> 00:42:02.150
are not that illustrative.

00:42:02.150 --> 00:42:04.310
Looking at the a
specific task help.

00:42:04.310 --> 00:42:06.250
But they are the--

00:42:06.250 --> 00:42:09.550
performance was near parity
relative to human experts.

00:42:09.550 --> 00:42:11.450
By duration, the
models were better

00:42:11.450 --> 00:42:13.810
at shorter tasks
up to a few hours,

00:42:13.810 --> 00:42:16.210
definitely declines
with longer tasks.

00:42:16.210 --> 00:42:18.750
And we already discussed
that on modality.

00:42:18.750 --> 00:42:20.750
There were some models
were better at multimodal

00:42:20.750 --> 00:42:23.410
while others were
better at text.

00:42:23.410 --> 00:42:26.110
OK, this is going to be
an extremely dense plot,

00:42:26.110 --> 00:42:28.650
but I think the thing to notice
here in all of these plots

00:42:28.650 --> 00:42:30.890
is that the red line is
the one where we are saying

00:42:30.890 --> 00:42:35.650
the human experts and then
all of these different colors

00:42:35.650 --> 00:42:37.610
are corresponding
to different models.

00:42:37.610 --> 00:42:41.490
So if you look at places
where the models are better

00:42:41.490 --> 00:42:44.830
or at least are almost at
par with the human experts,

00:42:44.830 --> 00:42:46.670
you see that counter
and rental clerks.

00:42:46.670 --> 00:42:49.530
So some of those tasks have
already started to hit that.

00:42:49.530 --> 00:42:52.130
Real estate brokers,
for example.

00:42:52.130 --> 00:42:55.710
Shipping, receiving, and
inventory clerks, for example.

00:42:55.710 --> 00:42:57.270
Buyers and purchasing agents.

00:42:57.270 --> 00:42:59.530
You can imagine why.

00:42:59.530 --> 00:43:02.290
Computer and IT
managers are definitely

00:43:02.290 --> 00:43:05.930
like it's starting to hit where
an average human expertise would

00:43:05.930 --> 00:43:06.730
be.

00:43:06.730 --> 00:43:10.170
When I say average this is
not like the best expert

00:43:10.170 --> 00:43:11.070
in the field.

00:43:11.070 --> 00:43:13.370
And then software
developers definitely are.

00:43:13.370 --> 00:43:15.850
A lot of the models are
starting to hit capability

00:43:15.850 --> 00:43:19.770
in typical software
engineering tasks.

00:43:19.770 --> 00:43:23.490
I'm seeing some
interesting reactions

00:43:23.490 --> 00:43:25.650
from students in class.

00:43:25.650 --> 00:43:27.690
Can I have some comments?

00:43:27.690 --> 00:43:30.710
For the software, I know that
you mentioned previously,

00:43:30.710 --> 00:43:34.970
it's like [INAUDIBLE]
14 years of experience.

00:43:34.970 --> 00:43:38.090
But software
developer, it can be--

00:43:38.090 --> 00:43:42.010
like the range can
be pretty wide.

00:43:42.010 --> 00:43:44.053
I think in the case of
software developers,

00:43:44.053 --> 00:43:45.970
they probably went for
more of the maintenance

00:43:45.970 --> 00:43:48.750
of the repository as opposed
to the decade of experience.

00:43:48.750 --> 00:43:50.403
I think there are
fields here where,

00:43:50.403 --> 00:43:53.070
I mean, for example, industrial
engineer or mechanical engineer,

00:43:53.070 --> 00:43:55.030
the decade of
experience matters.

00:43:55.030 --> 00:43:57.610
And software developer,
knowing the software

00:43:57.610 --> 00:43:59.370
codebase in and out.

00:43:59.370 --> 00:44:01.662
And the then two cases
of that [INAUDIBLE].

00:44:05.730 --> 00:44:07.810
OK, this is another
one of those thoughts.

00:44:07.810 --> 00:44:11.090
Let's just focus on where
we're seeing the models

00:44:11.090 --> 00:44:12.110
do extremely well.

00:44:12.110 --> 00:44:16.490
So again, government, the
administrative service managers,

00:44:16.490 --> 00:44:21.570
in compliance officers, models
are doing extremely well.

00:44:21.570 --> 00:44:24.430
And then in medical and
health service managers,

00:44:24.430 --> 00:44:27.010
those tasks, multiple models
are doing extremely well.

00:44:27.010 --> 00:44:29.890
In personal financial
advisor and customer service

00:44:29.890 --> 00:44:31.775
representatives
also, you're starting

00:44:31.775 --> 00:44:34.150
to see that the models are
starting to do extremely well.

00:44:34.150 --> 00:44:36.450
But in general, this
gives you a glossary

00:44:36.450 --> 00:44:39.530
or a taxonomy set of
tasks that the models are

00:44:39.530 --> 00:44:41.070
starting to perform well in.

00:44:41.070 --> 00:44:43.090
And if you're looking
at other tasks

00:44:43.090 --> 00:44:44.690
where the models
are failing, I think

00:44:44.690 --> 00:44:47.210
this is a very good way to
get a starting point on,

00:44:47.210 --> 00:44:48.830
what are the models failing at?

00:44:52.050 --> 00:44:54.970
Questions or comments?

00:44:54.970 --> 00:44:57.970
OK, this is the last
round, I promise.

00:44:57.970 --> 00:45:00.950
So first line supervisors
and retail sales workers.

00:45:00.950 --> 00:45:03.110
I think in general,
in retail trade,

00:45:03.110 --> 00:45:06.690
we are starting to see some
of those tasks taken on

00:45:06.690 --> 00:45:11.010
by the [INAUDIBLE] as a useful
thing for non-retail sales

00:45:11.010 --> 00:45:11.510
workers.

00:45:11.510 --> 00:45:13.430
So in general, on
the sales side.

00:45:13.430 --> 00:45:15.210
In terms of editing
tasks, we're seeing

00:45:15.210 --> 00:45:17.650
the models do extremely well.

00:45:17.650 --> 00:45:22.170
In terms of research-based
tasks, both investigative

00:45:22.170 --> 00:45:25.750
and detective-based
and news analysts,

00:45:25.750 --> 00:45:29.550
and then sales representatives,
wholesale and manufacturing,

00:45:29.550 --> 00:45:32.370
those tasks seem to
suggest that the models are

00:45:32.370 --> 00:45:34.210
doing extremely well.

00:45:34.210 --> 00:45:36.250
So this gives you a
flavor of the kind

00:45:36.250 --> 00:45:40.410
of tasks that the models
are performing strongly at.

00:45:40.410 --> 00:45:43.230
Overall, if you
under-specify the prompts.

00:45:43.230 --> 00:45:45.350
So if you actually remove
context from the prompt,

00:45:45.350 --> 00:45:49.210
then the models do end
up not doing that well.

00:45:49.210 --> 00:45:51.930
In their particular case,
there was a few points lower

00:45:51.930 --> 00:45:53.230
in terms of win rate.

00:45:53.230 --> 00:45:55.390
But in terms of
actual model output,

00:45:55.390 --> 00:45:58.310
the models actually struggle
to figure out what to work on.

00:45:58.310 --> 00:46:00.050
So there's definitely
some amount

00:46:00.050 --> 00:46:01.770
of-- when you're doing
real work and you

00:46:01.770 --> 00:46:05.370
can specify all the context
that the human had in their head

00:46:05.370 --> 00:46:06.590
to go do the work.

00:46:06.590 --> 00:46:09.730
And it's mostly about reasoning
and just going and executing

00:46:09.730 --> 00:46:12.330
a certain set of tool calls,
then the models can do the job.

00:46:12.330 --> 00:46:14.050
But there's a lot
of figuring out

00:46:14.050 --> 00:46:17.977
what needs to be worked on, how
that context needs to be used,

00:46:17.977 --> 00:46:19.310
what needs to be in the context.

00:46:19.310 --> 00:46:21.090
And a lot of that
doesn't get captured

00:46:21.090 --> 00:46:23.210
by the model on its own.

00:46:23.210 --> 00:46:27.130
So that's definitely one
area where the humans have

00:46:27.130 --> 00:46:28.790
to orchestrate, what is it?

00:46:28.790 --> 00:46:34.610
What is the actual
thing to go work on?

00:46:34.610 --> 00:46:36.730
So humans are
basically architecting,

00:46:36.730 --> 00:46:38.150
what is the set of problems?

00:46:38.150 --> 00:46:41.430
And then the model can go
solve it in certain cases.

00:46:44.370 --> 00:46:46.490
And this is further
exemplified by what

00:46:46.490 --> 00:46:49.530
we covered before where if you
had a repository maintainer

00:46:49.530 --> 00:46:51.650
versus an external
contractor, the speed at which

00:46:51.650 --> 00:46:54.010
the repository maintainer
is able to solve problems

00:46:54.010 --> 00:46:55.190
is generally much faster.

00:46:55.190 --> 00:46:57.840
So if you have an expert
who has a domain knowledge,

00:46:57.840 --> 00:47:00.700
they will generally
be faster compared

00:47:00.700 --> 00:47:02.400
to someone who has no context.

00:47:02.400 --> 00:47:05.288
So real work is
often context heavy.

00:47:05.288 --> 00:47:07.580
And what we are measuring in
this particular evaluation

00:47:07.580 --> 00:47:10.160
is more around, can a
smart person do this?

00:47:10.160 --> 00:47:12.600
Or can someone embedded
in this work can do this?

00:47:12.600 --> 00:47:15.900
But if you have to go
get context for that task

00:47:15.900 --> 00:47:17.720
that models are
not yet able to do,

00:47:17.720 --> 00:47:22.460
and this evaluation
is not measuring that.

00:47:22.460 --> 00:47:24.060
So in near term,
what this means is

00:47:24.060 --> 00:47:25.940
that AI assistance
is already happening

00:47:25.940 --> 00:47:27.120
in professional workflows.

00:47:27.120 --> 00:47:29.580
We have covered some of
the agentic workflows

00:47:29.580 --> 00:47:31.323
in early parts of the lecture.

00:47:31.323 --> 00:47:32.740
And it's definitely
cost effective

00:47:32.740 --> 00:47:34.407
when you couple it
with human oversight,

00:47:34.407 --> 00:47:36.900
like if the humans
are architecting what

00:47:36.900 --> 00:47:39.820
the models need to go work on,
the AI assistance is definitely

00:47:39.820 --> 00:47:41.220
helping.

00:47:41.220 --> 00:47:46.808
In certain occupations, this
is more impactful than not.

00:47:46.808 --> 00:47:48.600
But you do have to
choose different models,

00:47:48.600 --> 00:47:51.860
and different models are
stronger at different task types

00:47:51.860 --> 00:47:53.620
as well.

00:47:53.620 --> 00:47:58.200
OK, questions or comments.

00:48:02.020 --> 00:48:04.020
Yes.

00:48:04.020 --> 00:48:11.220
How much [INAUDIBLE] on the
actual dollar value [INAUDIBLE].

00:48:11.220 --> 00:48:12.260
So the tasks?

00:48:12.260 --> 00:48:15.632
Yeah.

00:48:15.632 --> 00:48:18.380
Why do you ask that question?

00:48:18.380 --> 00:48:25.860
Well, because it seems like it's
a relevant way of quantifying

00:48:25.860 --> 00:48:27.160
actual economic impact.

00:48:27.160 --> 00:48:32.880
You can say, oh, AI can now do
$100,000 of work or something

00:48:32.880 --> 00:48:34.420
that.

00:48:34.420 --> 00:48:38.140
But if these numbers
are collected

00:48:38.140 --> 00:48:46.860
in a way that seems unreliable
or misleading, then [INAUDIBLE].

00:48:46.860 --> 00:48:49.540
I think to me, it's not
so much about the dollar.

00:48:49.540 --> 00:48:53.380
I mean, they did go after
the top 5% of the GDP tasks.

00:48:53.380 --> 00:48:57.908
For the past several
decades, the knowledge work

00:48:57.908 --> 00:48:59.700
has been one of the
key areas that has been

00:48:59.700 --> 00:49:00.975
the highest paid profession.

00:49:00.975 --> 00:49:03.100
And in knowledge work,
there are certain categories

00:49:03.100 --> 00:49:06.340
of tasks that the models
will be very good at.

00:49:06.340 --> 00:49:09.740
Deep research being one of
them, perhaps, another one

00:49:09.740 --> 00:49:11.160
being like code generation.

00:49:11.160 --> 00:49:13.820
So in each-- or
understanding a diverse

00:49:13.820 --> 00:49:15.480
set of information sources.

00:49:15.480 --> 00:49:18.002
So the models will generally
be good at those set of tasks.

00:49:18.002 --> 00:49:19.460
So I think the
question I would ask

00:49:19.460 --> 00:49:23.585
is, what is the category of
tasks that the models are

00:49:23.585 --> 00:49:24.460
likely to be good at?

00:49:24.460 --> 00:49:26.428
Just because at any
given point in time,

00:49:26.428 --> 00:49:28.220
humans are strong at
certain set of things.

00:49:28.220 --> 00:49:30.380
And machines, just
by way of being

00:49:30.380 --> 00:49:34.718
able to crunch the
electricity, are just better

00:49:34.718 --> 00:49:35.760
at certain set of things.

00:49:35.760 --> 00:49:38.120
So this is more
along those lines.

00:49:38.120 --> 00:49:43.260
So there's a certain category
of tasks that will definitely

00:49:43.260 --> 00:49:44.460
be valuable.

00:49:44.460 --> 00:49:46.920
Delegating to the model
is the way to look at it.

00:49:46.920 --> 00:49:49.640
And which are the professions
in which those category of tasks

00:49:49.640 --> 00:49:50.140
fall?

00:49:50.140 --> 00:49:54.660
Is what this benchmark
is showcasing.

00:49:54.660 --> 00:49:57.060
Before this benchmark
came out, people

00:49:57.060 --> 00:49:59.533
would talk about this
area of like OK, this

00:49:59.533 --> 00:50:01.200
is going to impact a
lot of professions,

00:50:01.200 --> 00:50:02.580
but there was no
quantitative way

00:50:02.580 --> 00:50:05.400
to measure what set of tasks
in each of these professions.

00:50:05.400 --> 00:50:07.920
And it seemed like a
humongous task to go do that.

00:50:07.920 --> 00:50:10.860
So this is a very
verifiable, quantifiable way

00:50:10.860 --> 00:50:14.995
to even have a taxonomy
to list the set of tasks

00:50:14.995 --> 00:50:16.120
in each of the professions.

00:50:16.120 --> 00:50:19.420
And what does it mean for
the model to go do them?

00:50:19.420 --> 00:50:22.260
Earlier, when we
were doing the class,

00:50:22.260 --> 00:50:24.020
in the previous
iteration, we were only

00:50:24.020 --> 00:50:26.980
talking about ML-Bench and
RE- Bench and given that.

00:50:26.980 --> 00:50:29.980
Our backgrounds are, say, more
ML or more research or more

00:50:29.980 --> 00:50:30.940
software.

00:50:30.940 --> 00:50:35.140
It's easier for us to understand
the progress of these models

00:50:35.140 --> 00:50:36.520
in those domains.

00:50:41.140 --> 00:50:44.540
I'm just curious, how is
[INAUDIBLE] when the model will

00:50:44.540 --> 00:50:49.340
outperform the
human, it just means

00:50:49.340 --> 00:50:54.980
like it's human, not
necessary [INAUDIBLE] anymore.

00:50:54.980 --> 00:50:57.400
I mean, this is where I
was adding these caveats.

00:50:57.400 --> 00:50:59.880
So when a model is
outperforming the human,

00:50:59.880 --> 00:51:02.000
you should go look
at the prompt.

00:51:02.000 --> 00:51:04.580
And what that's saying
is that for a human who

00:51:04.580 --> 00:51:07.300
has a lot of
context, if they were

00:51:07.300 --> 00:51:09.020
to specify all the
context in their head

00:51:09.020 --> 00:51:11.940
to specify it in a
prompt, then the models

00:51:11.940 --> 00:51:15.300
can also do those tasks.

00:51:15.300 --> 00:51:20.020
While a lot of actual work
experience or a lot of workplace

00:51:20.020 --> 00:51:23.320
challenges often are about
figuring out what to prioritize,

00:51:23.320 --> 00:51:25.460
what are the set of
problems to solve,

00:51:25.460 --> 00:51:27.520
where to put our resources.

00:51:34.180 --> 00:51:37.900
We will move to the last
benchmark, which will be

00:51:37.900 --> 00:51:40.340
focused on DeepScholar-Bench.

00:51:40.340 --> 00:51:42.660
This was a task that
one of our classmates

00:51:42.660 --> 00:51:46.300
requested early on, where they
want AI to write the literature

00:51:46.300 --> 00:51:48.220
review section of their paper.

00:51:48.220 --> 00:51:49.560
So here we are.

00:51:49.560 --> 00:51:52.620
Let's benchmark that task.

00:51:52.620 --> 00:51:54.120
This was written at Stanford.

00:51:54.120 --> 00:51:58.020
And this is basically
measuring the question of,

00:51:58.020 --> 00:52:03.700
instead of asking how long
will the task take to solve,

00:52:03.700 --> 00:52:05.120
is the model output good enough?

00:52:05.120 --> 00:52:08.680
They're actually asking, can AI
perform deep research synthesis?

00:52:08.680 --> 00:52:10.900
Can I go study a
bunch of references

00:52:10.900 --> 00:52:13.160
and retrieve the
relevant information

00:52:13.160 --> 00:52:17.020
and then synthesize the findings
into a coherent set of reports

00:52:17.020 --> 00:52:19.080
and have verifiable citations?

00:52:19.080 --> 00:52:23.060
And there have been
several industry prototypes

00:52:23.060 --> 00:52:23.880
that have come out.

00:52:23.880 --> 00:52:26.240
So there's OpenAI deep
research, Gemini deep research.

00:52:26.240 --> 00:52:28.680
Perplexity has a
deep research thing.

00:52:28.680 --> 00:52:30.320
Storm is from Stanford.

00:52:30.320 --> 00:52:32.580
There is OpenScholar.

00:52:32.580 --> 00:52:35.420
You folks built a deep
research agent in homework 3

00:52:35.420 --> 00:52:38.500
and maybe you got a flavor
of what that feels like.

00:52:38.500 --> 00:52:40.900
So in general, there has been
a lot of research activity

00:52:40.900 --> 00:52:43.460
in this area, and it still
continues to be a challenging

00:52:43.460 --> 00:52:45.120
area, as what this paper shows.

00:52:49.140 --> 00:52:51.940
So the task that
this paper addresses

00:52:51.940 --> 00:52:55.600
is to generate the related work
section for academic papers.

00:52:55.600 --> 00:52:57.100
The reason they
care about this task

00:52:57.100 --> 00:53:00.180
is because this is a
task that most of us

00:53:00.180 --> 00:53:02.420
require when we
are writing papers.

00:53:02.420 --> 00:53:04.960
And it requires continuous
updates with fresh queries.

00:53:04.960 --> 00:53:07.060
So there is not no staleness.

00:53:07.060 --> 00:53:09.780
The way they went about
constructing this dataset

00:53:09.780 --> 00:53:13.900
is they used recent arXiv
papers with PhD level difficulty

00:53:13.900 --> 00:53:15.660
across 22 domains.

00:53:15.660 --> 00:53:20.760
And they rerun it monthly with
new papers, so it stays live.

00:53:20.760 --> 00:53:23.760
So it's very nice in
that respect as well.

00:53:23.760 --> 00:53:26.120
And it avoids data
contamination.

00:53:26.120 --> 00:53:27.940
They only look at
papers that are

00:53:27.940 --> 00:53:32.040
post-training of a major model.

00:53:32.040 --> 00:53:35.220
So that's very nice.

00:53:35.220 --> 00:53:37.260
What they're evaluating
the models on

00:53:37.260 --> 00:53:38.857
is three particular axes.

00:53:38.857 --> 00:53:40.940
So the first thing they're
evaluating the model on

00:53:40.940 --> 00:53:41.997
is knowledge synthesis.

00:53:41.997 --> 00:53:43.580
So what they're
basically saying that,

00:53:43.580 --> 00:53:46.820
is the model output
organizing and is

00:53:46.820 --> 00:53:49.520
coherent in organizing the
knowledge that is presenting?

00:53:49.520 --> 00:53:51.400
And is it capturing
all the key facts?

00:53:51.400 --> 00:53:54.500
So is it capturing
the key nuggets?

00:53:54.500 --> 00:53:58.180
This second axes, it's
evaluating the model

00:53:58.180 --> 00:53:59.900
on its retrieval quality.

00:53:59.900 --> 00:54:03.020
So if it's looking at a
particular set of references,

00:54:03.020 --> 00:54:07.160
how relevant they are to what
you wanted to do research on?

00:54:07.160 --> 00:54:09.100
For example, if you're
writing a related work

00:54:09.100 --> 00:54:15.200
section for your final
project, and you have say,

00:54:15.200 --> 00:54:20.480
10 references that this model
or agent went and fetched,

00:54:20.480 --> 00:54:22.580
are they relevant
to what you actually

00:54:22.580 --> 00:54:25.980
want to present in your
final report or not?

00:54:25.980 --> 00:54:27.620
Citation counts.

00:54:27.620 --> 00:54:30.480
It's kind of well known that you
want highly authentic sources.

00:54:30.480 --> 00:54:33.180
So whether the papers
are important in terms

00:54:33.180 --> 00:54:35.780
of covering those
in the retrieval

00:54:35.780 --> 00:54:37.620
and then reference coverage.

00:54:37.620 --> 00:54:39.220
So this is similar to--

00:54:39.220 --> 00:54:42.420
is it finding important
papers or not?

00:54:42.420 --> 00:54:46.650
And then the third axis it's
measuring is verifiability.

00:54:46.650 --> 00:54:49.490
So, do the citations
support the claims?

00:54:49.490 --> 00:54:52.790
So even if it finds
good papers and it's

00:54:52.790 --> 00:54:55.070
citing some aspect
of that paper,

00:54:55.070 --> 00:54:58.270
is the claim supported
by that citation?

00:54:58.270 --> 00:55:00.880
And then, are claims
backed by the citation?

00:55:00.880 --> 00:55:02.130
So these two are very similar.

00:55:02.130 --> 00:55:06.310
But one is measuring precision
and one is measuring coverage.

00:55:06.310 --> 00:55:10.890
And each of these metrics are
actually validated by humans.

00:55:10.890 --> 00:55:14.230
So they got about 70% to
80% of human agreement

00:55:14.230 --> 00:55:18.790
when they got humans
to evaluate this.

00:55:18.790 --> 00:55:21.182
Now the fun thing is that
most benchmarks get saturated.

00:55:21.182 --> 00:55:23.390
So when we look at benchmarks,
we are like, OK, well,

00:55:23.390 --> 00:55:25.410
it's already at 70% or 80%.

00:55:25.410 --> 00:55:26.450
So there is no headroom.

00:55:26.450 --> 00:55:28.325
The fun thing about this
particular benchmark

00:55:28.325 --> 00:55:32.290
is that none of the existing
systems actually exceeds 19%.

00:55:32.290 --> 00:55:34.350
So if this were to show
up in your homework

00:55:34.350 --> 00:55:36.050
for the next iteration
of the class,

00:55:36.050 --> 00:55:37.675
there's a lot of
headroom for you folks

00:55:37.675 --> 00:55:40.590
to actually make
it a final project.

00:55:40.590 --> 00:55:44.750
In terms of, if you look at
each of these axes on which they

00:55:44.750 --> 00:55:46.930
are breaking down the
model performance,

00:55:46.930 --> 00:55:48.630
in terms of knowledge
synthesis, they

00:55:48.630 --> 00:55:51.390
found that OpenAI deep research
does a pretty good job,

00:55:51.390 --> 00:55:55.368
but almost all of the models
consistently miss key facts.

00:55:55.368 --> 00:55:56.910
So even though they
write coherently.

00:55:56.910 --> 00:55:59.590
So it's great English,
but not necessarily

00:55:59.590 --> 00:56:01.870
covering all the key facts.

00:56:01.870 --> 00:56:07.070
The retrieval quality
tends to be overall OK.

00:56:07.070 --> 00:56:09.750
So all systems
currently struggle

00:56:09.750 --> 00:56:12.870
to find comprehensive and
important set of sources.

00:56:12.870 --> 00:56:17.670
The document importance in all
of them is less than 12.5%.

00:56:17.670 --> 00:56:20.970
And then finally, if you
look at verifiability,

00:56:20.970 --> 00:56:27.250
they found that say DeepScholar
base got up to 90% precision.

00:56:27.250 --> 00:56:29.850
But OpenAI deep research
has lower verifiability,

00:56:29.850 --> 00:56:31.830
despite the fact that it
does a really good job

00:56:31.830 --> 00:56:34.490
at writing really nice English.

00:56:34.490 --> 00:56:40.830
So the synthesis is
extremely coherent.

00:56:40.830 --> 00:56:42.490
Now, if you look at
the failure modes,

00:56:42.490 --> 00:56:46.230
and that's where a lot
of the learnings lie.

00:56:46.230 --> 00:56:48.710
Typically, the failure
modes come from not

00:56:48.710 --> 00:56:50.450
finding comprehensive sources.

00:56:50.450 --> 00:56:52.890
So even when they do
find relevant documents,

00:56:52.890 --> 00:56:55.050
they're not always finding
the foundational papers.

00:56:55.050 --> 00:56:57.370
So this is where if you
are an expert in the area,

00:56:57.370 --> 00:56:59.750
you kind of know from
experience that there

00:56:59.750 --> 00:57:02.190
are all these foundational
papers because you

00:57:02.190 --> 00:57:07.110
build that context and bookmark
all the important papers.

00:57:07.110 --> 00:57:09.930
The deep research agents
do a reasonably strong job,

00:57:09.930 --> 00:57:12.830
but they struggle to assess
the document importance

00:57:12.830 --> 00:57:16.550
beyond whether it's relevant
to the context or not.

00:57:16.550 --> 00:57:17.815
The surface essential facts.

00:57:17.815 --> 00:57:19.190
So even with good
retrieval, they

00:57:19.190 --> 00:57:22.930
don't always extract the
key information efficiently.

00:57:22.930 --> 00:57:25.950
If you do give the
perfect sources,

00:57:25.950 --> 00:57:28.190
so even if you say that
OK, here are all the papers

00:57:28.190 --> 00:57:29.730
that should have been included.

00:57:29.730 --> 00:57:33.070
Even then, if you try to get
them to extract the key facts,

00:57:33.070 --> 00:57:34.950
even then they get
about 50% coverage.

00:57:34.950 --> 00:57:39.230
And without knowing the exact
papers, they get lower coverage.

00:57:39.230 --> 00:57:41.670
And then finally, if you're
trying to balance out

00:57:41.670 --> 00:57:44.990
the synthesis quality
versus verifiability,

00:57:44.990 --> 00:57:47.230
none of the systems
actually excels

00:57:47.230 --> 00:57:50.270
at both the quality of
synthesis and the verifiability

00:57:50.270 --> 00:57:51.350
in certain ways.

00:57:51.350 --> 00:57:56.590
So you can see that being able
to refer to reference data

00:57:56.590 --> 00:58:00.250
in the agentic evaluation
tasks is a non-trivial problem.

00:58:00.250 --> 00:58:02.230
So it's not just about
how long the task is

00:58:02.230 --> 00:58:04.770
or whether it's real world
economic value or not.

00:58:04.770 --> 00:58:07.830
If you have to reference some
reference data to go look at,

00:58:07.830 --> 00:58:11.790
it's non-trivial because you
might not get the context

00:58:11.790 --> 00:58:12.730
that an expert has.

00:58:12.730 --> 00:58:14.990
So you might not have that
much foundational knowledge

00:58:14.990 --> 00:58:15.790
available.

00:58:15.790 --> 00:58:18.190
It might not extract the
key information well enough,

00:58:18.190 --> 00:58:19.990
so it does something.

00:58:19.990 --> 00:58:22.750
So it's good with
human AI assistance.

00:58:22.750 --> 00:58:25.630
And the verifiability
with respect to citations

00:58:25.630 --> 00:58:28.190
may not have the right precision
recall trade-off that's

00:58:28.190 --> 00:58:30.250
acceptable for that occupation.

00:58:33.590 --> 00:58:37.310
So that roughly means-- so this
is transitioning to summarize

00:58:37.310 --> 00:58:38.650
across all the papers.

00:58:38.650 --> 00:58:41.910
But before I go there, let
me open up for questions just

00:58:41.910 --> 00:58:46.910
on the DeepScholar-Bench
style of tasks.

00:58:46.910 --> 00:58:48.450
Any questions or comments?

00:58:53.390 --> 00:58:55.150
Yeah.

00:58:55.150 --> 00:58:58.750
[INAUDIBLE] seems like a much
more specific narrow thing

00:58:58.750 --> 00:59:01.490
than the other two.

00:59:01.490 --> 00:59:04.950
Why talk about this one
in particular as opposed

00:59:04.950 --> 00:59:08.430
to any other [INAUDIBLE]?

00:59:08.430 --> 00:59:10.750
So I think the broad
theme of the lecture

00:59:10.750 --> 00:59:15.310
was very much focused
on, what do we care about

00:59:15.310 --> 00:59:17.330
in agentic evaluations per se?

00:59:17.330 --> 00:59:19.930
So we covered about the
length of the tasks.

00:59:19.930 --> 00:59:22.270
We talked about the
economic value of the tasks.

00:59:22.270 --> 00:59:24.970
But for real-world
tasks to be meaningful,

00:59:24.970 --> 00:59:27.750
we do have to look at
knowledge bases in useful ways.

00:59:27.750 --> 00:59:31.170
So this is a representative
task for that category.

00:59:36.510 --> 00:59:40.330
Also for the most
foundational papers,

00:59:40.330 --> 00:59:42.410
so I guess like
for the benchmark,

00:59:42.410 --> 00:59:45.990
there's a list that's
collected from human experts.

00:59:45.990 --> 00:59:49.750
These are the papers
that people with the PhDs

00:59:49.750 --> 00:59:52.030
think the model should hit.

00:59:52.030 --> 00:59:55.990
And then you are trying to see
how many were actually retrieved

00:59:55.990 --> 01:00:01.830
or whatever like by
the model being tested.

01:00:01.830 --> 01:00:03.490
I mean, at any
given point in time,

01:00:03.490 --> 01:00:05.870
they're basically
saying yes, so there's

01:00:05.870 --> 01:00:09.510
the human aspect of here are the
list of papers that are there.

01:00:09.510 --> 01:00:12.030
But they're also looking
at whether it's--

01:00:12.030 --> 01:00:16.590
I mean, it's not just the
number of the set of papers.

01:00:16.590 --> 01:00:18.830
It's also talking about,
are you supporting

01:00:18.830 --> 01:00:21.240
if you want to make a set
of claims, when [INAUDIBLE]

01:00:21.240 --> 01:00:22.990
section, you're answering
the question of,

01:00:22.990 --> 01:00:24.510
how is this work related?

01:00:24.510 --> 01:00:26.010
So you want to make
a set of claims.

01:00:26.010 --> 01:00:28.090
My paper is different
from these other papers.

01:00:28.090 --> 01:00:30.930
And let's say they agree on a
set of topics with the model.

01:00:30.930 --> 01:00:32.650
You still have to make
a bunch of claims.

01:00:32.650 --> 01:00:34.400
And then you have to
look at the citations

01:00:34.400 --> 01:00:36.390
and back those claims
up and then say

01:00:36.390 --> 01:00:38.770
that your paper is different
in this particular regards.

01:00:38.770 --> 01:00:40.812
And then this is what
being covered by the field.

01:00:40.812 --> 01:00:44.310
So, even though the key
information coverage and then

01:00:44.310 --> 01:00:46.030
which papers get
referenced for those

01:00:46.030 --> 01:00:51.950
claims don't have sufficient
coverage in some ways.

01:00:51.950 --> 01:00:54.750
So it's both retrieval quality
and then what gets written down

01:00:54.750 --> 01:00:56.170
in terms of the key facts.

01:01:00.070 --> 01:01:01.050
Does that make sense?

01:01:03.590 --> 01:01:08.890
So let's try to summarize
across a different set of tasks.

01:01:08.890 --> 01:01:11.790
And this is kind of also
addressing the question

01:01:11.790 --> 01:01:13.990
that our classmate asked.

01:01:13.990 --> 01:01:19.350
So in the time horizon sense,
research synthesis tasks

01:01:19.350 --> 01:01:22.010
can be anywhere from
30 minutes to 8 hours.

01:01:22.010 --> 01:01:25.650
But the current capability range
is somewhere around 50 minutes.

01:01:25.650 --> 01:01:28.450
But the quality of this
horizon might still be poor.

01:01:28.450 --> 01:01:31.650
You might still end up
with, say, 50% success rate.

01:01:31.650 --> 01:01:35.150
So what this is roughly saying
is that if research synthesis

01:01:35.150 --> 01:01:38.450
tasks are in long
horizon in METR,

01:01:38.450 --> 01:01:40.490
and even though they
look good on that plot,

01:01:40.490 --> 01:01:45.030
they might not be high quality.

01:01:45.030 --> 01:01:48.290
In terms of economic
value, I mean,

01:01:48.290 --> 01:01:52.770
research synthesis tasks show
up in say financial research.

01:01:52.770 --> 01:01:56.990
They show up in a lot of
different research tasks.

01:01:56.990 --> 01:01:58.570
So they have clear
economic value,

01:01:58.570 --> 01:02:01.230
but they definitely
require capabilities

01:02:01.230 --> 01:02:03.330
that current models
are not always hitting.

01:02:03.330 --> 01:02:05.690
And oftentimes, the gaps
are coming from, say,

01:02:05.690 --> 01:02:09.070
multi-step reasoning, from being
able to do a comprehensive job

01:02:09.070 --> 01:02:12.510
at information gathering
to maintain verifiability

01:02:12.510 --> 01:02:14.410
while you're synthesizing
this information.

01:02:14.410 --> 01:02:16.050
So if you remember
from homework 3,

01:02:16.050 --> 01:02:18.230
when you basically
combine information

01:02:18.230 --> 01:02:20.170
from multiple
different agent calls,

01:02:20.170 --> 01:02:22.830
you still have to maintain a
certain amount of verifiability.

01:02:22.830 --> 01:02:25.548
And then you're
combining the context.

01:02:25.548 --> 01:02:27.590
So there's context
engineering from a distributed

01:02:27.590 --> 01:02:28.530
set of sources.

01:02:28.530 --> 01:02:30.447
Maybe you did a bunch
of different tool calls.

01:02:30.447 --> 01:02:33.130
Maybe you went and
redid the tool calls.

01:02:33.130 --> 01:02:35.350
So all of that
information means you

01:02:35.350 --> 01:02:37.630
have to maintain
the context in each

01:02:37.630 --> 01:02:40.270
of those multi-step scenarios.

01:02:40.270 --> 01:02:42.650
So we are not just
limited by task duration.

01:02:42.650 --> 01:02:46.350
How well we do in terms
of quality of synthesis

01:02:46.350 --> 01:02:49.030
at these time horizons
also has a lot

01:02:49.030 --> 01:02:50.450
of headroom for improvement.

01:02:53.390 --> 01:02:56.130
And, how does this vary
from a real world task?

01:02:56.130 --> 01:02:58.010
So, I mean, as I said before.

01:02:58.010 --> 01:02:59.650
So benchmarks are never perfect.

01:02:59.650 --> 01:03:01.550
They are a
representation of what

01:03:01.550 --> 01:03:04.150
we think is measurable today.

01:03:04.150 --> 01:03:07.910
And so METR is
automatically scored.

01:03:07.910 --> 01:03:09.530
There's no multi-agent
interaction.

01:03:09.530 --> 01:03:11.790
So it's basically
targeting a set

01:03:11.790 --> 01:03:15.350
of tasks that are single agent.

01:03:15.350 --> 01:03:16.892
And it's not
punishing of mistakes.

01:03:16.892 --> 01:03:18.350
And the resource
constraints, there

01:03:18.350 --> 01:03:21.590
is no resource constraints in
terms of how much resources

01:03:21.590 --> 01:03:22.290
can be used.

01:03:22.290 --> 01:03:25.410
So it's basically,
in real-world,

01:03:25.410 --> 01:03:28.510
you might actually have
more subjective constraints

01:03:28.510 --> 01:03:30.110
in how things are scored.

01:03:30.110 --> 01:03:32.030
You might be able to
break down the task

01:03:32.030 --> 01:03:34.120
into multiple agents,
which might actually

01:03:34.120 --> 01:03:36.315
lead to a better evaluation.

01:03:36.315 --> 01:03:38.440
And then there might be
actual resource constraints

01:03:38.440 --> 01:03:39.980
or cost of mistakes.

01:03:42.720 --> 01:03:44.400
In the second set
of paper that we

01:03:44.400 --> 01:03:46.440
covered called GDPVal,
which focuses very

01:03:46.440 --> 01:03:48.380
much on economically
valuable tasks,

01:03:48.380 --> 01:03:51.920
the key aspect there is
that the tasks are extremely

01:03:51.920 --> 01:03:54.640
well specified, all the
contexts that the human

01:03:54.640 --> 01:03:57.800
would have in their head
gets specified in the task.

01:03:57.800 --> 01:03:58.695
And it's one shot.

01:03:58.695 --> 01:04:00.320
So there is no
iterative back and forth

01:04:00.320 --> 01:04:03.920
where you get to fix
what the model did.

01:04:03.920 --> 01:04:06.280
And it requires
the model to have

01:04:06.280 --> 01:04:07.940
some amount of tacit knowledge.

01:04:07.940 --> 01:04:11.240
So it needs the knowledge to
be there already in the model.

01:04:11.240 --> 01:04:14.120
And that gap gets covered
more by the third benchmark

01:04:14.120 --> 01:04:16.920
that we were looking
at research synthesis.

01:04:16.920 --> 01:04:19.320
Here the challenges are
that the models still

01:04:19.320 --> 01:04:22.240
struggle with finding
comprehensive, high-quality

01:04:22.240 --> 01:04:26.300
sources and surfacing key
facts and then verifying them.

01:04:26.300 --> 01:04:29.280
And these are things that, if a
human is an expert in the field

01:04:29.280 --> 01:04:31.320
and does this day
in and day out,

01:04:31.320 --> 01:04:33.120
they'll be much faster
at because they just

01:04:33.120 --> 01:04:37.560
have that context in
their head all the time.

01:04:37.560 --> 01:04:40.280
So, what do we know now in
terms of model capabilities

01:04:40.280 --> 01:04:42.520
and where there's
headroom for improvement?

01:04:42.520 --> 01:04:44.600
So we kind of know
that the models

01:04:44.600 --> 01:04:47.260
have been improving on
isolated, well-specified tasks.

01:04:47.260 --> 01:04:50.280
If you have a set of tasks that
are well-specified and fall

01:04:50.280 --> 01:04:53.300
in the set of domains where
the models are good at,

01:04:53.300 --> 01:04:55.320
then they do a really good job.

01:04:55.320 --> 01:04:57.120
Software engineering
and ML research

01:04:57.120 --> 01:05:00.440
are two domains where we have
seen significant progress,

01:05:00.440 --> 01:05:03.330
perhaps because as computer
scientists and computer

01:05:03.330 --> 01:05:05.580
engineers, we tend to
understand those domains better.

01:05:05.580 --> 01:05:09.800
So we are automating
away our own jobs.

01:05:09.800 --> 01:05:12.720
So we definitely are
seeing these models

01:05:12.720 --> 01:05:17.160
do a really good job in terms of
solving hour long tasks there.

01:05:17.160 --> 01:05:20.360
And the stronger models
can generate very well

01:05:20.360 --> 01:05:23.680
organized outputs, even if
not all parts of that outputs

01:05:23.680 --> 01:05:25.240
are correct.

01:05:25.240 --> 01:05:27.040
What we have lower
confidence in,

01:05:27.040 --> 01:05:28.560
if you look at
model capabilities,

01:05:28.560 --> 01:05:34.120
is that the model's
performance on tasks where you

01:05:34.120 --> 01:05:35.660
need a lot of context is low.

01:05:35.660 --> 01:05:38.860
So if you don't have the
context in the model's prompt,

01:05:38.860 --> 01:05:41.200
then the model doesn't quite
know what to go work on.

01:05:41.200 --> 01:05:45.220
It doesn't quite figure
out ambiguous prompts,

01:05:45.220 --> 01:05:48.440
and just figure out what
it needs to figure out

01:05:48.440 --> 01:05:50.400
to solve the problem.

01:05:50.400 --> 01:05:53.740
The performance in
adversarial environments

01:05:53.740 --> 01:05:55.880
is not necessarily great.

01:05:55.880 --> 01:05:58.580
Overall, if you want
95% reliability,

01:05:58.580 --> 01:06:00.040
the models are not quite there.

01:06:00.040 --> 01:06:03.840
And generally, we have not yet
seen a lot of generalization

01:06:03.840 --> 01:06:05.920
beyond software
and knowledge work.

01:06:05.920 --> 01:06:10.260
Knowledge work encompasses a lot
of the digital workloads today.

01:06:12.880 --> 01:06:15.760
We also think that the
models are still not

01:06:15.760 --> 01:06:19.080
strong at finding comprehensive,
high quality sources when they

01:06:19.080 --> 01:06:23.400
have to reference knowledge
bases and surface key facts,

01:06:23.400 --> 01:06:25.520
and then verify
what they surfaced

01:06:25.520 --> 01:06:29.000
as high accuracy and high
coverage in the knowledge base.

01:06:29.000 --> 01:06:34.560
So all of these are still gap
areas in model capabilities.

01:06:34.560 --> 01:06:38.160
So if we were to summarize
across the three papers

01:06:38.160 --> 01:06:41.300
that we talked about
today, in METR,

01:06:41.300 --> 01:06:44.200
we talked about the
seven-month doubling horizon

01:06:44.200 --> 01:06:46.400
in terms of model capabilities.

01:06:46.400 --> 01:06:52.920
As in the models can complete
the tasks with 50% reliability.

01:06:52.920 --> 01:06:55.900
That time horizon has been
doubling every seven months.

01:06:55.900 --> 01:06:59.380
And the forecast is
that say, 2028 to 2031,

01:06:59.380 --> 01:07:01.080
it will almost be a one month.

01:07:01.080 --> 01:07:05.040
Task can be completed
by the model.

01:07:05.040 --> 01:07:07.600
But that view gets
contradicted in some ways

01:07:07.600 --> 01:07:10.040
by GDPVal win rate,
which shows more

01:07:10.040 --> 01:07:13.040
of a linear improvement
over the last two years,

01:07:13.040 --> 01:07:16.000
where even though the win rate
for a certain set of tasks

01:07:16.000 --> 01:07:19.480
is 48%, at the
same time, you will

01:07:19.480 --> 01:07:21.200
see that there are
many category of tasks

01:07:21.200 --> 01:07:23.360
that don't have high win rates.

01:07:23.360 --> 01:07:25.400
And then the other
gap area that you see

01:07:25.400 --> 01:07:27.440
is that a lot of
the tasks in GDPVal

01:07:27.440 --> 01:07:29.480
actually require
context or require

01:07:29.480 --> 01:07:30.880
looking at reference data.

01:07:30.880 --> 01:07:33.640
And DeepScholar-Bench, the
research synthesis task

01:07:33.640 --> 01:07:36.120
kind of shows you that
there are critical gaps when

01:07:36.120 --> 01:07:38.120
you have to go retrieve
some set of context

01:07:38.120 --> 01:07:39.460
or look at a knowledge base.

01:07:39.460 --> 01:07:41.720
And generally, the quality
and the verifiability

01:07:41.720 --> 01:07:45.000
don't quite match
up compared to what

01:07:45.000 --> 01:07:48.080
a human would be able to do.

01:07:48.080 --> 01:07:51.920
So in general, the reliability
and the error recovery

01:07:51.920 --> 01:07:53.700
of the model should
continue to improve,

01:07:53.700 --> 01:07:57.320
but the performance will
vary still over task types,

01:07:57.320 --> 01:08:00.000
like which category of
tasks we are looking at.

01:08:00.000 --> 01:08:03.215
So even if the models are
solving longer and longer

01:08:03.215 --> 01:08:04.840
horizon tasks, that
doesn't necessarily

01:08:04.840 --> 01:08:07.640
mean they're always giving
highly reliable or quality

01:08:07.640 --> 01:08:08.180
outputs.

01:08:08.180 --> 01:08:12.600
So we need metrics which
are duration-based, economic

01:08:12.600 --> 01:08:14.900
value-based, and also
synthesis quality-based.

01:08:14.900 --> 01:08:17.479
And you still need to
validate them against humans

01:08:17.479 --> 01:08:20.479
who can solve these tasks.

01:08:20.479 --> 01:08:23.340
OK with that, I will actually
open up for questions.

01:08:27.560 --> 01:08:31.560
If you go back to the first
task for the [INAUDIBLE] team.

01:08:31.560 --> 01:08:39.560
So what do you think
about the [INAUDIBLE]

01:08:39.560 --> 01:08:43.359
it's similar to many
like Moore's law.

01:08:43.359 --> 01:08:47.439
So I'm just curious because
you are much experts than me.

01:08:47.439 --> 01:08:50.560
What do you think [INAUDIBLE]?

01:08:50.560 --> 01:08:52.700
So I think there's two
answers to your question.

01:08:52.700 --> 01:08:54.500
I mean, SWE-bench
definitely shows--

01:08:54.500 --> 01:08:56.200
SWE-bench Verified,
for example, has

01:08:56.200 --> 01:08:58.319
shown a really strong growth.

01:08:58.319 --> 01:09:02.319
In general encoding
area, we will

01:09:02.319 --> 01:09:03.899
continue to see improvements.

01:09:03.899 --> 01:09:05.399
And still there is
a large category

01:09:05.399 --> 01:09:11.460
of tasks which are not just
pull request-based and so on.

01:09:11.460 --> 01:09:12.779
And these are hard tasks.

01:09:12.779 --> 01:09:17.560
So there's a long path that
will continue to be challenging

01:09:17.560 --> 01:09:18.840
for the longest.

01:09:18.840 --> 01:09:22.540
I mean, Waymo, the first
[INAUDIBLE] easy to do,

01:09:22.540 --> 01:09:24.740
and then the last
20% [INAUDIBLE].

01:09:28.640 --> 01:09:30.720
Distributed systems
being one of the areas

01:09:30.720 --> 01:09:33.800
in which computer science, where
it's very hard for the model

01:09:33.800 --> 01:09:34.700
to get it right.

01:09:38.880 --> 01:09:44.800
You've mentioned a couple
times automating [INAUDIBLE].

01:09:44.800 --> 01:09:52.680
What is your sense of how you
expect your own work to look

01:09:52.680 --> 01:09:54.724
different two years from now?

01:09:57.840 --> 01:10:02.440
So I think AI timelines are
moving extremely fast in ways

01:10:02.440 --> 01:10:06.680
where it's very hard to predict
something one year from now.

01:10:06.680 --> 01:10:09.040
I think one of my
tasks would end up

01:10:09.040 --> 01:10:11.920
being [INAUDIBLE] what
an AI scientist does,

01:10:11.920 --> 01:10:15.200
and AI scientists being able to
come up with hypotheses and run

01:10:15.200 --> 01:10:19.200
experiments and then complete
the whole loop is still

01:10:19.200 --> 01:10:23.080
something of-- that is
our definition of AGI

01:10:23.080 --> 01:10:26.620
is to when AI can build the next
generation of models themselves,

01:10:26.620 --> 01:10:29.600
then we don't have to
even be in the loop.

01:10:29.600 --> 01:10:32.720
But I don't think that
is quite there yet.

01:10:32.720 --> 01:10:35.120
But it's definitely
been fascinating

01:10:35.120 --> 01:10:37.000
to see the level of
progress in terms

01:10:37.000 --> 01:10:39.600
of how the AI models do
have the knowledge base

01:10:39.600 --> 01:10:44.280
and often are extremely
strong brainstorming partners.

01:10:44.280 --> 01:10:46.240
So as AI co-scientist
at this point

01:10:46.240 --> 01:10:53.360
in time, which has a better
value for a lot of the use

01:10:53.360 --> 01:10:57.520
cases, is what I see.

01:10:57.520 --> 01:11:01.360
I also generally feel
that in most areas,

01:11:01.360 --> 01:11:04.120
getting reliability or the
long tail of reliability

01:11:04.120 --> 01:11:06.700
is going to be
extremely challenging.

01:11:06.700 --> 01:11:09.960
So that's the area in
which the last mile

01:11:09.960 --> 01:11:12.300
is hard to get progress on.

01:11:16.520 --> 01:11:19.600
I thought more of a
clarification question

01:11:19.600 --> 01:11:21.480
about the beta paper.

01:11:21.480 --> 01:11:26.210
So they talk about this concept
of 50% pass completion rate.

01:11:26.210 --> 01:11:30.690
I was wondering if the 50%
is like an average success

01:11:30.690 --> 01:11:33.250
rate, maybe, let's
say, let's say they're

01:11:33.250 --> 01:11:36.050
like 100 test instances.

01:11:36.050 --> 01:11:39.850
And then for each test
instance, AI will try 10 times.

01:11:39.850 --> 01:11:42.370
And then you see you count
how many times that it

01:11:42.370 --> 01:11:43.970
fails or succeeds.

01:11:43.970 --> 01:11:48.390
And then you take the average
over the 100 times 10,

01:11:48.390 --> 01:11:52.530
or is it more like you want to
be make sure for every task,

01:11:52.530 --> 01:11:54.810
it has at least
50% because I feel

01:11:54.810 --> 01:11:57.810
like the latter would be much
more stringent and harder

01:11:57.810 --> 01:11:58.570
to achieve.

01:11:58.570 --> 01:12:00.270
I think it's multiple
attempts for sure,

01:12:00.270 --> 01:12:02.330
because if you remember,
we showed this plot.

01:12:02.330 --> 01:12:04.770
So there are multiple agent
runs and it's basically

01:12:04.770 --> 01:12:09.730
saying how many times it
completed successfully.

01:12:09.730 --> 01:12:12.050
So you have maybe--
so, yeah, so here

01:12:12.050 --> 01:12:13.990
let's say there are 97 tasks.

01:12:13.990 --> 01:12:15.570
So each task, you
would calculate

01:12:15.570 --> 01:12:18.230
a success rate of the agent.

01:12:18.230 --> 01:12:22.930
And then you take the average
of the individual task success

01:12:22.930 --> 01:12:26.610
rate across the 97, is
that what you think they're

01:12:26.610 --> 01:12:29.730
doing to get the final 50%?

01:12:29.730 --> 01:12:32.990
So yes, roughly, right?

01:12:32.990 --> 01:12:36.850
For each horizon, they're
basically drawing these points.

01:12:36.850 --> 01:12:38.690
And the points are--

01:12:38.690 --> 01:12:40.850
the success rate
for each task is

01:12:40.850 --> 01:12:46.170
coming from what percentage
of attempts were successful.

01:12:46.170 --> 01:12:49.170
So 50% success rate
for a particular task

01:12:49.170 --> 01:12:51.850
roughly would mean that
50% of the time, it

01:12:51.850 --> 01:12:54.830
might come back with a
good answer, 30% maybe not.

01:12:54.830 --> 01:12:56.150
So it's a flip of a coin.

01:13:07.890 --> 01:13:13.190
[INAUDIBLE] so AI is
doing much better.

01:13:13.190 --> 01:13:17.178
So it's [INAUDIBLE]
engineering job [INAUDIBLE].

01:13:20.490 --> 01:13:22.630
OK, let's take that
off for the class.

01:13:25.730 --> 01:13:27.890
I think the computer science
as a profession is not

01:13:27.890 --> 01:13:29.450
going anywhere.

01:13:29.450 --> 01:13:31.930
And if you are able to
reason for fundamentals,

01:13:31.930 --> 01:13:35.010
that is a very
important skill to have.

01:13:35.010 --> 01:13:39.010
AI, as you saw, needs to
figure out the context

01:13:39.010 --> 01:13:39.830
or solve anything.

01:13:39.830 --> 01:13:43.530
So you need to be able
to give it the context.

01:13:43.530 --> 01:13:45.590
So that's my belief.

01:13:52.820 --> 01:13:54.590
All right, I have a question.

01:13:54.590 --> 01:14:01.470
What do you think are stopping
AI from tackling those long tail

01:14:01.470 --> 01:14:01.970
problems?

01:14:01.970 --> 01:14:03.010
Is it lack of data?

01:14:03.010 --> 01:14:09.030
Or what else, I mean, what's
the fundamental limitations?

01:14:09.030 --> 01:14:12.590
It's a mix of both a data
problem and a fundamental model

01:14:12.590 --> 01:14:13.610
capabilities.

01:14:13.610 --> 01:14:16.945
In certain cases, if you--

01:14:16.945 --> 01:14:18.570
I think there are
several startups that

01:14:18.570 --> 01:14:22.390
are tackling things and building
environments and whatnot,

01:14:22.390 --> 01:14:25.570
but coming up with tasks
that are representative

01:14:25.570 --> 01:14:28.530
and then stopgap in the
model capabilities in each

01:14:28.530 --> 01:14:31.850
of these tasks continues to be
an area which is challenging

01:14:31.850 --> 01:14:32.830
at this point in time.

01:14:32.830 --> 01:14:36.370
So I think when they are
broad across categories

01:14:36.370 --> 01:14:39.350
like, say, legal research
or financial research,

01:14:39.350 --> 01:14:43.710
those have seen much faster
adoption and improvements.

01:14:43.710 --> 01:14:45.390
But when they're
very, very specific,

01:14:45.390 --> 01:14:49.852
like having enough
data to or having

01:14:49.852 --> 01:14:52.310
the model progress in the right
model capabilities is hard.

01:14:52.310 --> 01:14:55.170
For example, if you look at
robotics as a domain, which

01:14:55.170 --> 01:14:57.210
is the embodied
AI stuff, there's

01:14:57.210 --> 01:15:00.330
a whole suite of
startups that are just

01:15:00.330 --> 01:15:03.370
going after data collection
as the primary hypothesis

01:15:03.370 --> 01:15:06.350
to close the gap based
on top of these models.

01:15:08.930 --> 01:15:10.730
OK.

01:15:10.730 --> 01:15:12.560
Thank you.

